# Metal–Organic Frameworks for CO2 Separation from Flue and Biogas Mixtures

## abstract
Great efforts have been made toward the separation of CO2 from flue gas and biogas to mitigate environmental pollution and the demand for renewable fuels, respectively. Nonthermal‐based separations, such as adsorption‐based or membrane‐separation technology employing porous materials, are considered to be more promising than traditional cryogenic and absorption‐based systems. Due to several advantages of metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) over other conventional porous materials, reports on flue and biogas separation by MOFs are burgeoning (423 for adsorption and 56 for membrane‐based separations until June 2021) and demand urgent summarization. This review presents a bird's eye view on such separations while organizing the developed strategies and considering several performance parameters, such as trade‐off between sorption capacity and separation selectivity in adsorption‐based systems and permeability versus separation selectivity in membrane‐based systems. In addition, the mechanisms involving such separations at the molecular level are presented. A critical discussion section offers more crucial insights into these materials from industrial deployment viewpoints. Finally, future recommendations are made for further developments of MOF materials as flue and biogas separators and thus toward solving both the challenging universal problems of global warming and energy scarcity simultaneously.

## Metal–Organic Frameworks for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ Separation from Flue and Biogas Mixtures
Dedicated to Professor Sujit K. Ghosh
Great efforts have been made toward the separation of $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ from flue gas and biogas to mitigate environmental pollution and the demand for renewable fuels, respectively. Nonthermal-based separations, such as adsorption-based or membrane-separation technology employing porous materials, are considered to be more promising than traditional cryogenic and absorption-based systems. Due to several advantages of metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) over other conventional porous materials, reports on flue and biogas separation by MOFs are burgeoning (423 for adsorption and 56 for membrane-based separations until June 2021) and demand urgent summarization. This review presents a bird’s eye view on such separations while organizing the developed strategies and considering several performance parameters, such as tradeoff between sorption capacity and separation selectivity in adsorption-based systems and permeability versus separation selectivity in membrane-based systems. In addition, the mechanisms involving such separations at the molecular level are presented. A critical discussion section offers more crucial insights into these materials from industrial deployment viewpoints. Finally, future recommendations are made for further developments of MOF materials as flue and biogas separators and thus toward solving both the challenging universal problems of global warming and energy scarcity simultaneously.
## 1. Introduction
Due to the incredible population expansion, rapid urbanization, and industrial revolution, a huge amount of energy is regularly consumed, and the energy demand is tremendously increasing day by day. Needless to say, ${\approx}85\%$ of the required deliverable global energy demand is acquired from the burning of fossil fuels, in which approximately $24.2\%$ , $27\%$ , and $33.1\%$ come from natural gas, coal, and oil, respectively, i.e., nonrenewable energy resources, which are the prime anthropogenic sources of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ emissions and create severe environmental issues.[1,2] The global $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ emissions due to combustion of fossil fuel not only reached more than double since the 1970s but also exceeded 32 gigatonnes (Gt) of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ per year.[3] According to the
On the other hand, biogas, mainly composed of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ $(40-75\%)$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ $(15-60\%)$ (Table  1, Figure 1), is a renewable energy resource (such as wind and solar) and is usually produced from anaerobic digestion (AD) using regionally available wastes. While raw biogas can be used in various sectors, such as in i) $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production, ii) electricity and power generation, by combining heat and power units (CHP), and iii) heat and steam production units, the processed biogas, i.e., after upgradation and purification, it can also be used as i) injection into natural gas (NG) grids for its usual utilization in a wide range of sectors, such as the power, industrial, residential, commercial, and transportation sectors (accounting for $36\%$ , $33\%$ , $16\%$ , $11\%$ , and $3\%$ usage of total U.S. NG consumption, respectivey, in 2019),[10] ii) as a vehicular fuel in upgraded and compressed form. However, its commercial utilization is still limited due to the necessity of on-site purification and upgradation before its use and the difficulty faced in transporting it through pipelines. The presence of a large amount of noncombustible $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ $(15-60\%)$ in raw biogas composition accompanied by small amounts of several incondensable gases, such as oxygen $(\mathrm{O}_{2},0{-}1\%)$ , nitrogen $(\mathrm{N}_{2},0{-}2\%)$ , ammonia $(\mathrm{NH}_{3},~<1\%)$ , hydrogen sulfide $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ , $0.005\mathrm{-}2\%)$ , carbon monoxide (CO, $<0.6\%$ ), volatile organic compounds (VOC, $<0.6\%$ ), and water $(5-10\%)$ (Table 1), are the major culprits and need to separate from raw biogas.[11] The upgraded and purified version of biogas is called biomethane, which contains $95\mathrm{-}97\%$ $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ with a very low amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ $(1-3\%)$ and can be used as an alternative resource for natural gas. Transforming biogas to biomethane requires two steps: i) cleaning harmful trace components such as $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ , $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ , CO, etc.; ii) biogas upgradation process by separating $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to adjust the relative density and calorific value to meet the Wobbe index specifications (Figure 2).[11] As the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ content is reduced in the upgraded biogas, the relative density of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ decreases, and as a consequence, the calorific value increases, which further increases the Wobbe index. Upgraded biogas possesses a high calorific value of $35{-}44\mathrm{kJ\g^{-1}}$ , similar to several conventional fuels, such as kerosene, diesel, and LPG.[12] Most importantly, this calorific value is also higher than that of nonrenewable energy resources, such as coal and wood. The widespread usage of biogas in the generation/cogeneration of electricity, heat and steam, and vehicle fuel will be an “environmentally friendly” approach and will also significantly reduce the dependency and consumption of nonrenewable fossil fuels as they emit a huge amount of anthropogenic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . Considering this, the biogas industry is abruptly growing globally, and it is estimated that the usage of biogas will be doubled in the upcoming years, ranging from 14.5 gigawatts (GW) in 2012 to $29.5\mathrm{GW}$ in 2022 in the world.[13,14] According to the IRENA (International Renewable Energy Agency) statistical report, the global electricity generation from biogas was 46108 GWh in 2010, which sharply increased $90\%$ in just 6 years and reached 8 $7~500~\mathrm{GWh}$ in 2016.[15,16] Similarly, the Indian biogas energy capacity statistical report (2009–2020) showed that the biogas energy capacity doubled in the last six years, reached 14 megawatts in 2020 from 7 megawatts (in 2014), and increased tremendously over the years.[17] As the calorific power in biogas is solely dependent on its $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ content, to hold on the high calorific value of biogas, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ separation and purification is on demand. It is also recommended that the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ concentration be higher than $90\%$ (preferably $97-99\%)$ for internal combustion engines, as up to $50\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ concentration in biogas led to significant reduction in the engine power output and signifies the importance of biogas separation.[18] Moreover, in upgraded biogas, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ content $(\%\mathrm{vol})$ is necessarily $<4$ for vehicle fuel and $\leq3$ for natural gas grid to obtain the maximum potentiality.[12] In addition, the presence of acid compounds and impurities in biogas are corrosive in nature and have the potential to generate corrosive compounds during biogas combustion, which often affect the metal parts of internal combustion engines and tubing. Therefore, before entering corrosive compounds in natural gas grids or combustion engines, biogas purification is an important and mandatory step to ensure that the overall system is free from corrosion. Hence, to purify and upgrade raw biogas, several technologies have been employed, such as absorption, adsorption, cryogenic separation, and membrane separation, among which, except physisorption (physical adsorption), others are neither cost-effective nor energy-agreeable. Even though the compositions of flue and biogas are different, both of the gas mixtures deal with anthropogenic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas, and the common target is to separate $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from both mixtures. Although flue gas separation mainly focuses on $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ removal in regard to environmental issues, biogas separation aims to solve the energy scarcity problem. Concerning these, researchers have reinforced their immense attention toward effective designs and the development of easily regenerable advanced porous functional materials, which will not only show efficient $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture through the physisorption method from both flue and biogas mixtures but also control their separation performance by tailoring the architectures on a molecular level to achieve the trade-off between high sorption capacity and excellent separation selectivity. This review addresses such a newer type of porous material known as metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), which have been heavily explored for their immense potential in flue and biogas separation in the last twenty years. Although our focus in this review is mainly on developed microporous MOFs employed for adsorption-based flue and biogas separations, we have also comprehensively discussed the MOF-based mixed matrix membranes (MMMs) employed for such separations considering the importance, necessity and development of such membranebased systems. By a thorough survey, we found 56 examples of MOF-MMMs projected toward either flue and/or biogas separations. However, this number is significantly less than that of the developed MOF materials for adsorption-based separations of flue and biogas and thus demands extensive attention in the near future.
2. Why MOFs and Where Do They stand?
Currently, the benchmark carbon capture technology for flue gas separation addresses aqueous amine scrubbing and has been used in industrial applications for more than 60 years, which is very energy intensive, and the required parasitic energy for the removal of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is ${\approx}0.2–0.5\ \mathrm{MWh}$ ton−1.[19] This required parasitic load is equivalent to $20\text{\textperthousand}$ of the power plant output, whereas the regeneration of the absorbent also consumes more than $50\%$ of the overall energy.[19] The high energy penalty not only makes the overall process very energy consuming and cost intensive, but the use of basic amine solution also leads to corrosion of the equipment and generates pollutants in the environment. Needless to say, this technology also results in the increment of electricity cost by $25\mathrm{-}40\%$ and limits its widespread usage.[20,21] On the other hand, for biogas separation, high-pressure water scrubbing (HPWS), organic physical scrubbing (OPS), inorganic solvent scrubbing (ISS), amine scrubbing (AS), membrane separation (MS) and cryogenic separation (CS) were employed previously. While HPWS and OPS operate through methods based on physical absorption, ISS and AS are examples of typical absorption-based methods.[12,22] Although HPWS technology can provide $597\%$ $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ concentration in biogas upgradation, the major disadvantages are the requirement of high investment and operating costs and the requirement of huge amount water even in regeneration. Additionally, slow and less efficient processes along with clogging issues due to bacterial growth are also major issues in this process. [23] Similarly, OPS technologies demand high investment and operational costs to deliver upgraded biogas. Moreover, this process is very expensive for small-scale applications and requires high energy to regenerate the solvent. On the other hand, the major drawbacks for ISS and AS processes are the requirement of high capitalization, treatment of the waste chemicals, contaminant build-up, corrosion, and amine breakdown. Although membrane separation is relatively appealing for biogas upgradation, the separation process occurs through dissolution and diffusion into polymer membranes; thus, the major disadvantage in this process is the requirement of multistep separations to reach high purity due to lower membrane selectivity. The conventional cryogenic separation process requires very high pressure (approximately 80 bar) and low temperatures (near $\approx-170\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ throughout the distillation process, and separation occurs based on the liquefaction principle of different gases under different pressure–temperature conditions. Additionally, a large amount of instruments and equipment, such as turbines, compressors, distillation columns, and heat exchangers, are needed, which makes the overall separation process energy and capital intensive. In addition to all these separation techniques, adsorption-based techniques have also been employed,[24] which are relatively pronounced over others, and developing such technologies in terms of economic and environmental viability to minimize the energy penalty and reduce cost intensity for efficient $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ separation is highly desired and on-demand. To reduce regeneration energy in the gas sorption and separation process, the employment of this adsorption method is not only one option but also widely deemed to be a promising future technology, as it solely depends upon the energy-efficient temperature swing adsorption (TSA) process and pressure swing adsorption (PSA) process.[25,26] Among the several technologies mentioned above, the physisorption method using solid porous materials stands out magnificently, where the potential gas sorption and separations for any material underscore the necessity of using the differences in their chemical and physical properties such as kinetic diameters, polarization, quadrupole moment, and so on.[27] In adsorption-based processes (PSA and TSA), adsorption occurs through solute transfer to the surface in the gas stream of an adsorbent material driven by physical or van der Waals forces and has several advantages, such as simple operations, a high regeneration rate, high performance at ambient temperatures, cost-effectiveness and low energy intensity. Moreover, for this type of selective adsorption of targeted gas molecules over other competing gas molecules, three mechanisms are mainly considered: i) the size-exclusion principle, ii) kinetically controlled, and iii) thermodynamically controlled.[10] The first two separation mechanisms can be controlled via effective tuning of the pore diameters, whereas thermodynamic separation can be amplified via modulation of the adsorbents’ interiors with strong binding moieties, which will help in selective capture of targeted gas molecules and further will lead to the separation of gas mixtures. For selective and efficient capture of targeted $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules over competing gas molecules $(\Nu_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}\mathrm{,}$ ) in the flue and biogas separation, two points need to be considered: first, the deployment of renewable functional material that can be synthesized easily in a cost-effective manner, and second, the developed material can be sustained in practical separation conditions and can be marketized in a very short time. In this regard, researchers are keen to develop novel materials that will be cost-effective, highly scalable, and easily regenerable and will exhibit excellent efficiency in both sorption and separation performance. Since the mid-1990s, a large number of traditional solid adsorbents, such as activated carbons,[28–30] carbon nanotubes,[31–34] clays and oxides,[35–38] microporous zeolites,[39–42] mesoporous organic networks,[43–45] and molecular sieves,[46,47] have been employed for effective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture for flue and biogas separation. For example, in 1995, Cho et al. employed activated carbon and zeolite 13X for flue gas separation, in which zeolite 13X displayed much higher equilibrium IAST separation selectivity (∼1700) than activated carbon (∼1400).[48] In 1996, Sircar et al. developed zeolite 5A and BPL carbon, among which zeolite 5A displayed Henry Law flue and biogas separation selectivity of 330.7 and 195.6 at $303\mathrm{~K},$ respectively, whereas BPL carbon exhibited flue and biogas separation selectivity of 11.1 and 2.5, respectively.[49] $\mathrm{In}\ 2002$ , Radosz et  al. employed AC2 (activated carbon 2) for flue gas separation, which displayed a separation selectivity of 10.3 at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under 1 bar pressure.[50] In the same year, Kluson and Scaife et al. checked the potential of activated carbon AX21 for biogas separation, which displayed a separation selectivity of 8 at $293\mathrm{~K~}$ under 1  bar.[51] Along the same line, in 2013, Rios et  al. constructed activated carbon WV150, displaying biogas separation selectivity of 8.7 at $293\mathrm{~K~}$ under 1 bar.[52] In the same year, Moghadassi et al. developed a polymer-loaded multi-walled carbon nanotube, PVC/SBR/C-MWCNT- $.5\%$ , which displayed flue and biogas separation selectivity of 34.7 and 62.73, respectively, under 2  bar pressure.[53] Even though activated carbons and carbon nanotubes possess low cost and are also widely available, which makes them fascinating for this flue and biogas separation direction through adsorption-based separation, their relatively lower performance at low pressure restricts their potential.[49] Furthermore, in 2015, Ho et  al. analyzed the Pebax/zeolite Y composite’s performance toward flue gas separation, which exhibited a separation selectivity of 30 at $57^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .[54] Along the same line in 2014, Denayer et al. developed Rb- and Cs-ZK-5 zeolites, which displayed a biogas separation selectivity of 17 at $303\mathrm{~K~}$ under 1 bar pressure. Moreover, their separation potentiality was also analyzed through real dynamic breakthrough separation.[55] Ultem 1000/HSSZ-13 zeolite hollow fiber displayed a biogas separation selectivity of 46.9 at $35~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $7.8\ \mathrm{atm},^{[56]}$ whereas PVA/PEG blended zeolite 5 exhibited a biogas separation selectivity of 33 at $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 10 atm.[57] PES (polyethersulfone) polymer-incorporated zeolites 3A, 4A, and 5A exhibited biogas separation selectivity ranging from 32 to 44 at $35~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 10 atm.[58] Even though zeolites displayed moderate flue and biogas separation selectivity, high sensitivity to moisture not only reduces the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake significantly but also restricts them in practical separation employment.[59,60] Similarly, CaO, $\mathtt{M g O}$ , and FeO oxides were also capable of efficiently capturing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules, but they normally not only need to handle at high temperatures but also require calcination reactors.[59] As these adsorbents demand handling at high temperature $(>500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , the high temperature often leads to sintering of oxides and dramatically reduces their $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture performance.[61] Employing such materials for flue and biogas separation is undoubtedly a good choice, but they are inefficient in fulfilling the desired level due to their limited potential. Several restrictions in pore size tunability, modulation in surface functionality, and the high-temperature thermal activation process are the major obstacles in their potentiality. Systematic tuning of the pore size of developed porous materials is the key for selective gas sorption and separation through the adsorption method, as the fine-tuned pore size can effectively trigger the pore confinement effect. In this platform, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), composed of metal ions linked with organic ligands, are documented as a fascinating class of highly crystalline porous materials and have claimed considerable attention owing to their distinctive features and versatility,[62–86] including ultrahigh surface areas (beyond $7000\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ along with high porosity and low crystal density, as well as high chemical and thermal stability. [87–101] The advantages of MOFs arose from their superiority in surface modification,[102–105] effective pore modulation[106–110] and low- to moderate-temperature thermal activation,[111–114] which kept it upper hand for practical deployment toward this challenging separation direction over other conventional previously developed porous materials. These advantages of MOFs sparked interest in a vast range of potential applications involving gas storage and separation,[10,115–124] proton conductivity,[2,125–135] sensing,[136–142] luminescence,[143,144] heterogeneous catalysis,[145–152] and so on.[153–166] Specifically, the investigation of MOFs toward flue gas ( $15\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $85\%$ ${\mathrm{N}}_{2}^{\prime}$ ) and biogas $50\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $50\%\mathrm{CH}_{4}\mathrm{)}$ mixture separation has overgrown in recent years (Figure 3) over other potential application domains, and tremendous efforts have already been devoted to the development of MOFs to comprehend a better trade-off between high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity and separation selectivity. For selective gas sorption and separation, pore confinement plays a crucial role and can be governed by tailoring the pores via three prime approaches: i) the isoreticular approach, ii) the pore space partition (PSP) approach, and iii) framework interpenetration, as exclusively discussed in our recent review.[10] As depicted in Table 2, the main components of flue and biogas ( $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ ) possess partially similar physicochemical properties, which further not only make this separation process challenging but also demand an effective design and finely tuned framework to obtain maximum potentiality. From the above discussion, it is clear that MOFs are capable of sorting out this challenging separation, and based on that, the number of developed MOFs is also increasing swiftly (Figure 3), which demonstrates the necessity and emergency of MOF development over their other counterparts. It is known that flue gas and biogas contain varying amounts of humidity depending upon the source ( $5-7\%$ and $7\mathrm{-}18\%$ for coal-fired power plants and natural gas postcombustion process flue gas, respectively, whereas $5-10\%$ for biogas),[9,11] and thus, separation performance should be considered in the presence of humidity. Fortunately, a large number of MOFs retain their original pore structures in the presence of humidity (highly kinetically stable MOFs) and, in many cases, even in liquid water, boiling water or over a wide pH range (thermodynamically stable MOFs).[90,92,101] Such robust MOFs have shown great promise for flue and biogas separations when moisture is introduced in simulated gas mixtures. It has been found that many MOFs retained almost unaltered flue and biogas separation performance in the presence of humidity over multiple cycles. These findings are indeed remarkable when considering them to make a transition from academia to industry.
3. Why to Review on Flue and Biogas Separations?
Undoubtedly, flue gas separation is indeed necessary for solving pollution issues and saving the environment, whereas to reduce the extensive usage of nonrenewable energy resources and increase the calorific value of $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ biogas separation is indispensable and demands immense attention. To date, more than 70  000 MOFs have been developed, and the interesting fact is that the maximum number of developed MOFs has been projected toward selective gas sorption and separation application domains rather than any other application domains. Even though MOFs are now projected toward various gas separation applications (such as flue and biogas separations, various types of hydrocarbon separations, inert gas separations, etc.), to date the examples of flue and biogas separations by MOFs are the maximum one and also increasing swiftly day to day, as depicted in Figure 3. Previously, the potentiality of MOFs for challenging flue and biogas separations was investigated only by computational methods. However, now the focus has been shifted solely toward developing potential ultrarobust MOF materials, which will sustain their potentiality even in practical industrial platforms. Hence, the potentiality investigation is now fully based on experimental studies. In addition, it is very inspirational that some studies also have been performed experimentally with the partnership of simulation or theoretical studies, which is very necessary and demanding too for understanding and visualizing the structure-property relationship at the molecular levels. Additionally, there are quite a few developed MOFs where some in situ experiments have been performed to elucidate the sorption mechanism deeply, but unfortunately, such examples are very rare and need to be explored further in the future.
By scrupulous literature surveys, even though we have found quite a few review papers and book chapters where prime focuses have been devoted to the efficient capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules,[1,167–180] relatively less attention has been given to the summarization of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ separation from gas mixtures. In short, reviews solely based on flue $(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2})$ and biogas $\mathrm{(CO_{2}/C H_{4})}$ separations are still missing in the scientific community. For example, Abnisa et  al. recently reviewed different MOF synthesis procedures and compared them along with a short discussion of some MOF examples that captured $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules but did not specifically discuss flue or biogas mixture separation with any selectivity values or related information.[177] Similarly, Li et al. published a review paper focusing on $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ captures and provided some examples for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capturing
Hence, this thorough analysis reveals the scope of writing a review solely based on flue and biogas separation, keeping the prime focus on developed strategies, selectivity reasons and the respective selectivity values. By a scrupulous literature survey, we found 423 developed MOFs (through June 2021) that have been employed in flue $(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2})$ and/or biogas $\mathrm{(CO_{2}/C H_{4})}$ separations. Such a huge number of developed MOFs projected toward this challenging separation direction in a very short time fueled us to organize and summarize them for swift visualization. Herein, in this review, we aimed to organize them based on developing strategies and discuss them comprehensively, which will surely be helpful not only for at a glance visualization but also for further developments. However, the MOFs whose potentiality toward flue and biogas separations were analyzed solely by computational methods are beyond the scope of this review. In this review, our focus is fully on those developed MOFs whose potentiality has been checked either only experimentally or both experimentally and theoretically. In addition to the effective design and development of MOFs for challenging flue and biogas separation, understanding the sorption mechanism at the molecular level is equally important; thus, we have provided a separate section upon the elucidation of the sorption mechanism considering several in situ experiments, which will surely enrich the understanding level of the readers. Finally, we have provided a critical analysis section aimed at offering deep insight into the developed MOFs and correlating them from a practical industrial employment point of view. Additionally, we have clearly pointed out what should be our ultimate goal for the effective design and further development of efficient MOFs to obtain an excellent trade-off between sorption capacity and separation selectivity. To end, we have provided a clear discussion of several underdeveloped issues that need to be seriously considered for successful deployments of MOFs in flue and biogas industrial setups.
## 4. Classification of Strategies Taken for Flue and Biogas Separations
As mentioned earlier, through the judicious choice of organic ligands and by varying the metal centers, a large number of MOFs can be designed and employed them for selective gas separation applications by carefully observing their structural architecture and framework porosity. From the very beginning to the present, considerable effort has been made to study the developed MOFs’ potentiality toward flue and biogas separations by employing several strategies. Interestingly, it has been observed that for all cases, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake is significantly higher than that of other competing gas molecules $(\Nu_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}^{\cdot}$ ). Of course, the relatively smaller kinetic diameter of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (Table 2) is beneficial for efficient pore confinement over others within the micropores of MOFs. Additionally, the larger polarizability along with higher quadruple moments of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule (Table  2) over $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ are responsible for the enhanced host-guest interaction through quadrupole–dipole interactions between the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule and the polar MOF surface. By thorough investigation, we found that eight different strategies (Figure 4) have been taken thus far by researchers to realize flue and biogas separation performance for MOFs and optimize the trade-off between separation selectivity and sorption capacity, which are as follows.
i)	 Selectivity triggers through pore confinement a)	 Driven by framework interpenetration b)	 Driven by framework noninterpenetration   
ii) Selectivity triggers through open metal sites (OMSs)   
iii) Selectivity triggers through surface functionalization a)	 Driven by functional groups only b)	 Driven by bare heteroatoms c)	 Driven by multiple functional sites   
iv) Selectivity triggers through charged skeleton   
v) Selectivity triggers through framework flexibility (gateopening behavior)   
vi) Selectivity triggers through the pore space partition (PSP) approach   
vii) Selectivity triggers through the combined approach a)	 OMSs and surface functionalization b)	 OMSs and charged skeleton c)	 Surface functionalization and charged skeleton d)	 Bared functional sites and pore confinement
viii)	 Selectivity triggers through composite formations
In this review, we organized and classified the developed MOFs from 2010 to 2021 (until June) based on the strategies taken and discussed comprehensively with their suitable examples and corresponding figures (few more examples are also discussed in the Supporting Information).[182–458] We have tabulated the BET surface area, selectivity values and experimental conditions while pointing out the key features for achieving such selectivities for individual MOF systems in Table 3, Table 4, Table S1 and Table S2 (Supporting Information) for at a glance visualization. Table 3 addresses some of the representative MOFs discussed in this review, where both the flue gas and biogas separations have been studied together, while a comprehensive table is provided in the Supporting Information (Table S1). On the other hand, Table 4 lists representative developed MOFs where the potentiality has been tested either for flue gas or biogas separation, while the comprehensive table is placed in Table S2 of the Supporting Information.
## 4.1. Selectivity Triggers Through Pore Confinement
The proper tuning of porosity is very necessary and demanding too, as highly porous MOFs are incapable of efficiently capturing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules at low pressure even though they are potentially relevant for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ storage at high pressure. Our ultimate goal is not only to develop MOFs for the efficient capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas molecules over others but also an excellent tradeoff between the sorption capacity and separation selectivity for practical industrial employment. Hence, the development of MOFs with tuned pore sizes is on demand. For flue and biogas separation, the pore size range should be in the ultramicropore region because an appropriate pore size can only enhance separation selectivity through the pore confinement effect. For this purpose, the proper choice of organic ligand is very crucial to obtain a desirable MOF architecture. The use of a small ligand generally provides a small pore size, and it is expected that a larger pore size can be obtained by using a relatively larger ligand. Although a MOF with a larger pore can take up higher amount of gas molecules, it may exhibit a relatively poor selectivity because of the lack of pore confinement. Basically, for larger porous MOFs, the pore confinement effect becomes unworkable. However, the interesting fact is that the use of relatively larger ligands often gives rise to an interpenetrated framework with a relatively smaller pore size, as obtained for smaller ligands, and thus displays a significant effect on gas separation through pore confinement. Even though both the interpenetrated and noninterpenetrated frameworks are capable of pore confinement, there is an extra advantage for the interpenetrated framework, i.e., due to the framework interpenetration, the stability of the framework increases significantly. Thus, we have divided this category “Selectivity triggers through pore confinement” into two parts, a) “Driven by framework interpenetration” and b) “Driven by framework noninterpenetration,” and discussed comprehensively with suitable examples.
## 4.1.1. Driven by Framework Interpenetration
Considering the pore confinement effect through framework interpenetration, in 2019, Mandal et  al. developed a microporous doubly interpenetrated robust 3D MOF, $\{[\mathsf{C o}_{2}(4,4^{\prime}$ -bpy) (L)] $\cdot_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}.0.5(\mathrm{DMF})\}_{n},$ (1), which possessed two types of open pore channels (rhombic and trigonal) with dimensions of $\bar{5}.4\times7.0\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ and $1.7\times2.5\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ (Figure 5).[203] Even though interpenetrated framework 1 exhibited modest uptake of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules (51.3 and $36.4~\mathrm{cc~g^{-1}}$ at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and $298~\mathrm{K}_{:}$ respectively), it displayed flue gas $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (v/v 15:85)] separation selectivity of 194.7 and 70.5 at 1  bar pressure and $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ temperatures, respectively (Table  3, Sl. No 24), as obtained via IAST calculations. However, the biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ $\left(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}~50{:}50\right)]$ separation selectivity was 19 and 9.9 at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ temperatures under 1  bar pressure, as tabulated in Table  3 (Sl. No 24). The higher $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity and separation selectivity could be ascribed to the pore confinement effect due to the presence of suitable pore channels driven by framework interpenetration. Additionally, the higher affinity of the framework toward $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules could be attributed to the relatively smaller kinetic diameter $(3.30\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}})$ than the other competing components $(\Nu_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}^{\cdot}$ ) and having a higher quadrupole moment (Table  2), which allowed easy diffusion of the sieved $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules. Additionally, the framework exhibited high chemical robustness even after treatment with water for a month and boiling water for two days, which could be attributed to the framework interpenetration and the presence of paddle-wheel $\{\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{4}\}$ SBU (secondary building units) and metal-N-coordination throughout the framework. This work demonstrated the significant impact of framework interpenetration, which not only triggered high separation selectivity through pore confinement but also highlighted the viability for practical flue and biogas separation applications by enhancing framework robustness and made the “selectivity triggers through pore confinement (driven by framework interpenetration)” approach feasible.
Recently, in 2019, our group also developed a 3D microporous 2-fold interpenetrated Co-MOF, IITKGP-11, possessing 1D open channels of $3.6\mathrm{~\AA~}\times5.0\mathrm{~\AA~}$ along the [1 0 1] direction suitable for the selective capture of targeted $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules over competing $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ through pore confinement.[187] Although activated IITKGP-11 displayed moderate saturated uptake capacity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas molecules $(60.5~\mathrm{cc~g^{-1}}$ at 295 K), the IAST selectivity tabulation displayed a flue gas $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ $(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}~15{:}85)]$ separation selectivity of 149.07 at $295\mathrm{~K~}$ (Table 3, Sl. No 6). The biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ $\left(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}~50{:}50)\right]$ separation selectivity was 7.91 at ambient temperature and pressure, which indicated the efficiency of IITKGP-11 toward both flue and biogas separations. To validate the real $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ separation performance, real-time dynamic breakthrough experiments were performed. The high breakthrough retention time of 64 and 37  min for targeted $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules of flue and biogas separation, respectively, proved the excellent selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture potentiality of IITKGP-11 under practical circumstances. The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ loading amounts of the mixed gas phase in IITKGP-11 were 0.92 and 1.73 mmol $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ for the flue and biogas mixtures, respectively, as obtained from the dynamic breakthrough study. Interestingly, IITKGP-11 retained its potentiality up to four breakthrough cycles without any loss of separation performance, which highlighted the outstanding recyclability and easy regenerablity of the developed framework (Figure 6a). Furthermore, to deduce the supramolecular host-guest interactions and determine preferential binding sites of the captured $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules within the framework, a GCMC simulation study was performed, which showed that the electropositive O atom of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ strongly interacted with the electronegative pyridyl hydrogens of the organic spacer through $\mathbf{C}{\cdot}\mathbf{H}\cdot\mathbf{\cdot}\cdot\mathbf{O}=\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{O}$ electrostatic interactions with an average distance of $3.91\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ (Figure 6b). Therefore, this study demonstrated the effectivity of interpenetrated MOF construction to lead to high flue and biogas separation selectivity despite having moderate uptake capacity.
Along the same line, Cheng et  al. developed a 2-fold interpenetrated 3D framework, $\{\vert\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{CPT})_{4}(\upmu_{3}-$ $\left.\mathrm{OH}\right)\right]\cdot\mathrm{NO}_{3}\cdot7\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\cdot\mathrm{EtOH}\right\}_{n},$ termed 1, which exhibited a high flue gas, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\:(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}\:15{:}85)$ , separation selectivity of 105 at room temperature driven by pore confinement.[206] Interestingly, two positionally disordered CPT ligands (4-(4-carboxyphenyl)-1,2,4- triazole) in 1 were organized after activation at $353\mathrm{~K~}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ which indicated the transformation of a more ordered phase even after activation (1c) than the as-synthesized phase (1), as analyzed by SCXRD structural analysis. This phenomenon could be attributed to the stronger interframework interactions driven by framework interpenetration (Figure 7a). The pore diameter was analyzed by Poreblazer software, indicating a maximum pore diameter of $6.32\mathrm{~\AA~}$ An abrupt increase in the uptake of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at low pressure could be attributed to the strong host-guest $\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)$ interaction with framework dynamicity to some extent. IAST separation selectivity calculation revealed that 1c exhibited selectivity values of 105 for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ $(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}15{:}85)$ and 36 for an equimolar $(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}50{:}50)$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ binary mixture (Table 3, Sl. No 26, Figure 7b) at ambient temperature and 1 atm pressure. Thus, this study nicely established the significant impact of framework interpenetration in triggering the pore confinement effect and made this “selectivity triggers through pore confinement (driven by framework interpenetration)” approach viable for efficient flue and biogas separation applications.
As real flue and biogas separation often contains moisture, which may degrade the framework and impede the actual potentiality, the developed material must be moisture stable. Considering this, in 2017, Vaidhyanathan et  al. developed a 2-fold interpenetrated microporous Ni-MOF, IISERP-MOF2, with excellent moisture and hydrolytic stability.[454] IISERPMOF2 displayed a remarkable flue gas [ $\langle\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2},\ \mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}\ 15/85\rangle$ separation selectivity of 1853 at $313\mathrm{~K~}$ under 1 bar (Table 4, Sl. No 49) with selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture $(85.1\mathrm{~cc~g^{-1}})$ over $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (negligible uptake). The selective capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules could be attributed to the efficient pore confinement effect driven by framework interpenetration having a pore size of $4.7\mathrm{~\AA~}$ as obtained by GCMC simulation and DFT study. Interestingly, this MOF exhibited excellent framework robustness even in $85\%$ RH exposure for 7 days and 6 months exposure in open air $\mathrm{'RH}>65\%)$ (Figure 8a). Additionally, the 15 consecutive cycles of continuous assessment of $303\mathrm{~K~CO}_{2}$ sorption revealed no significant loss in uptake capacity (Figure 8b), which further not only proved the excellent recyclability and easy regenerability but also provided sufficient evidence for structural robustness. Furthermore, to examine the sorption kinetics, an experimental breakthrough study for the flue gas binary mixture was performed in both dry and wet conditions $50\%$ RH), as displayed in Figure  8c, d. Due to the strong binding affinity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules compared to competing $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ molecules, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules retained inside the packed column ${\sim}100~\mathrm{min}$ for dry and ${\sim}40\ \mathrm{min}$ for wet gas mixtures with an initial elution of high purity $\Nu_{2}$ . Most importantly, this MOF displayed only an approximately $7\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity loss (3.97 to $3.68\mathrm{~mmol~g^{-1})}$ , even under $50\%$ RH (Figure  8d), which signified the excellent retention of performance even in humid conditions.
## 4.1.2. Driven by Framework Non-Interpenetration
As discussed in the previous section, framework interpenetration is helpful in achieving efficient separation selectivity. On the other hand, the judicious choice of relatively smaller ligands can also provide suitable channel dimensions with noninterpenetrated structures for pore confinement, which can trigger separation selectivity, as pioneered by several groups. Such noninterpenetrated frameworks capable of showing flue and biogas separation through pore confinement are exemplified in this section.
To display how suitable pore size can efficiently trigger pore confinement, in 2016, Zaworotko et al. constructed five MOFs, Qc-5-M-dia and Qc-5-Cu-sql- $\alpha$ (where $\mathtt{M}=\mathtt{C u}$ , Co, Ni and $Z\mathrm{n}$ ), among which Qc-5-Cu-dia and Qc-5-Cu-sql- $\alpha$ possessed 1D pore channels with pore sizes of $4.8\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and $3.8\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , respectively.[224] Interestingly, after desolvation at $130~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , Qc-5-Cu-sql- $\alpha$ exhibited phase transformation and generated Qc-5-Cu-sql- $\beta$ with a decreased pore size (from $3.8\mathrm{~\AA~}$ to $3.3\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ) (Figure 9a), which was suitable for the efficient capture of selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules through pore confinement. The IAST selectivity calculation displayed remarkably high flue [ $\mathrm{(CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ , v/v 15/85)] and biogas [ $\mathrm{(CO_{2}/C H_{4},}$ v/v 50/50)] separation selectivity of $40\:000$ and 3300, respectively, at $293\mathrm{~K~}$ for Qc-5-Cu-sql- $\beta,$ as tabulated in Table 3 (Sl. No 35–37). Additionally, Qc-5-Cu-sql- $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\beta$ displayed excellent hydrolytic stability not only in water for 21 days but also in $75\%$ RH for 14 days, which could be attributed to the presence of coordinatively saturated Cu metal centers. To analyze the practical separation performance, a dynamic breakthrough experiment was performed not only for dry $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ (v/v 15/85) and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ (v/v 50/50) binary mixtures but also for a wet $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (v/v 15/85) mixture $(75\%$ RH), as depicted in Figure 9b. The significantly higher breakthrough time (Figure  9b) for dry and wet flue gas mixtures (121 and $112{\mathrm{~min}}$ , respectively) with an initial elusion of competing gas molecules proved the efficiency of Qc-5-Cu-sql- $\cdot\beta$ in real-time flue and biogas separations. A molecular simulation study displayed better close-fitting of host-guest $\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)$ interactions with a significantly shorter $\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})\cdots\mathrm{H}$ distance of $2.5\textup{\AA}$ for Qc-5-Cu-sql- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\beta$ (Figure 9c, d), whereas the distance was relatively larger $(>3.0\mathring\mathrm{A})$ for Qc-5-Nidia, Qc-5-Cu-dia and proved the potentiality and necessity of finely tuning the pore size in this direction. Hence, this study nicely displayed the efficiency of the pore confinement effect despite having framework noninterpenetration and made this “selectivity triggers through pore confinement (driven by framework noninterpenetration)” approach viable for the flue and biogas separation direction.
In 2015, Furukawa et  al. constructed four hexatopic linkerbased porous noninterpenetrated MOFs, MOF-888-891, possessing suitable pore channels for the selective capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas molecules and proved the pore confinement effect toward flue gas separation.[444] Among these four, MOF-890 exhibited the maximum BET surface area (Table 4, Sl. No 42) and exhibited selective saturated $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture of $58~\mathrm{cc~g^{-1}}$ with a negligible uptake of $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ $6{\mathrm{~cc~g}}^{-1}$ only) at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ under 800 torr pressure. Henry law selectivity tabulation revealed a maximum flue gas separation selectivity of 63 for MOF-890 at ambient temperature, whereas other MOF selectivity values were 57, 25, and 56 for MOF-888-889 and MOF-891, respectively (Table 4, Sl. No 40–43). As MOF-890 displayed maximum Henry separation selectivity despite having modest uptake, the practical separation ability was further analyzed through a room temperature dynamic breakthrough experiment, as depicted in Figure 10a. Due to strong host-guest $\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)$ interactions, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules were retained in the packed column in the pores for more than 100  sec, whereas competing $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ gas was eluted at the initial stage and proved the practical potentiality of the framework. Additionally, three consecutive breakthrough cycles of MOF890 displayed no remarkable loss in separation performance (Figure  10b), which further demonstrated the recyclability of the framework and made this material practicable for flue gas separation.
Recently, in 2021, Tian et  al. constructed a microporous 3D MOF, Cu-1, which not only possessed suitable micropores for the efficient capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules to lead high separation selectivity but also exhibited excellent acid-alkali resistivity $\mathrm{(pH~}2\mathrm{-}13)$ .[232] Single-component gas sorption measurements under ambient conditions (298 K) displayed selective capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules $(129\mathrm{~cc~g^{-1}})$ over other competing gas molecules (26 and $9.81~\mathrm{cc~g^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\mathrm{N}}_{2}^{\prime}$ ) at 1  bar and proved the efficiency of pore confinement. The $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ uptake was 5 and 13 times lower than that of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules, which indicated a strong host-guest interaction between $\mathrm{Cu-1}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules. To analyze the preferential binding interaction of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules throughout the framework, an AIMD (ab initio molecular dynamics) simulation study was performed, which displayed a strong ${\mathrm{C-H}}\cdot\cdot\cdot{\mathrm{O}}$ interaction between the host framework and the O of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules (Figure 11a). The ${\mathrm{C-H}}\cdot\cdot\cdot{\mathrm{O}}$ interacting distance was $2.52\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ smaller than the sum of O $(1.52\textup{\AA})$ and H (1.20 Å) atoms’ van der Waals radii of $2.72\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , and evident the strong host-guest interaction, which further led to the high sorption capacity. Furthermore, IAST separation selectivity calculations revealed flue $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ $\left(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}~15/85\right)\protect$ 1 and biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ $\left(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}50{:}50\right)]$ separation selectivities of 22 and 9 at 298 K under 1 bar (Table 3, Sl. No 41). Additionally, to verify the feasibility of $\mathrm{Cu-1}$ in practical gas purification processes, a dynamic breakthrough experiment was performed for binary mixtures of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ $(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}~50{:}50)$ , which displayed clear separation between $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ with a breakthrough retention time of $39~\mathrm{min}$ and initial elution (600 s) of competing $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules (Figure  11b). This study demonstrated a high selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity along with separation selectivity through pore confinement and suggested practical application prospects in biogas separation processes.
In 2017, Chen et  al. developed a 3D NbO-type Cu-MOF, UTSA-90, isostructural with NOTT-101.[196] The presence of bulky methoxy groups $\left(\mathrm{-OCH}_{3}\right)$ pointing to the pore channels in UTSA-90 significantly reduced the pores with a limiting radius of ${\sim}3.5\mathrm{~\AA~}$ suitable for the confinement of targeted $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules and thus exhibited significantly enhanced flue and biogas separation selectivity compared with NOTT-101. The benchmark MOF, NOTT-101, possessed pore diameters of 11.2 $\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ and $9.6\times22.3\mathrm{~\AA}^{2}$ , which was reduced effectively in UTSA-90 due to the presence of a bulky methoxy functionality with a decreased pore size of 11.2 to $8.9\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ along with a limiting radius of ${\sim}3.5\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , suitable for efficient $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture (Figure 12a). The coverage-dependent sorption enthalpy $(Q_{\mathrm{st}})$ calculation revealed a relatively lower $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ $\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ value for UTSA-90 (20.5 kJ mmole–1) than NOTT-101 (23.3  kJ $\mathrm{mmol^{-1}})$ , which further signified that the immobilization of ${\mathrm{-OCH}}_{3}$ groups in UTSA-90 did not offer additional strong sorption sites but generated desirable suitable pore sizes through the partition and dramatically enhanced the selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture over other competing gas molecules $\mathrm{(CH_{4}}$ and ${\mathrm{N}}_{2}^{\prime}$ ). The saturated $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\Nu_{2}$ uptakes were ${\sim}5$ and 18 times less (26 and $6.9\csc\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ than $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules at $295\mathrm{~K~}$ under 1 bar and displayed the efficiency of surface functionalization for selective targeted gas molecule capture. Furthermore, at $295\mathrm{~K~}$ under 1  bar, the IAST separation selectivity tabulation revealed high flue $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (v/v 15:85)] and biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ $\mathrm{(v/v~50{:}50)}]$ separation selectivities of 21.7 and 5.3 (Table  3, Sl. No 11) in UTSA-90 than the pristine NOTT-101 (13.8 and 4.7, respectively) (Figure 12b, c). Hence, this study demonstrated that even though methoxy $\mathrm{(-OCH}_{3})$ surface functionalized groups are incapable of creating additional binding sites, they significantly fine-tuned the pore sizes for efficient pore confinement and enhanced the selective capture of targeted gas molecules, which further made this approach more promising in this direction.
To verify the positional effect of methyl $\left(\mathrm{-CH}_{3}\right)$ functionality in pore confinement, in 2021, He et  al. developed four ligand-induced MOF isomers, ZJNU-26-29, with methyl substituents at different positions (Figure 13a).[200] The positional variability of methyl substituents not only triggered hydrostability but also exhibited a significant impact on framework flexibility, sorption capacity and separation selectivity. The high sorption capacity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules over other competing components $(\Nu_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4})$ could be attributed to the relatively smaller kinetic diameter and larger polarizability along with higher quadruple moments (Table  2). IAST selectivity calculations of ZJNU-26-29 exhibited the maximum $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ (v/v 15:85) and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ (v/v 50:50) binary mixture separation selectivity for ZJNU-29 (Table  3, Sl. No 17–20) at room temperature and 1  bar pressure, whereas selectivity decreased in the order of $\mathrm{ZJNU{-}}29~>~\mathrm{ZJNU{-}}28~>$ $\mathrm{ZJNU}{-}26>\mathrm{ZJNU}{-}27$ (Table 3, Sl. No 17–20, Figure 13b, c). Such a change in flue and biogas separation selectivity values could be attributed to the positional alteration of methyl $\left(\mathrm{-CH}_{3}\right)$ functionality, which triggered steric hindrances. As water molecules are often present as a common interference for such a separation process, the hydrostability of the developed framework is also crucial. Interestingly, ZJNU-27 and ZJNU-28 exhibited excellent hydrostability, which was confirmed by postexposure PXRD and BET surface area analyses. This work demonstrated that consideration of the positional effect of the bare functional group in pore confinement is also indispensable to obtain the maximum separation ability of the developed MOFs.
## 4.2. Selectivity Triggers Through Open Metal Sites (OMSs)
As discussed in the previous approach, a suitable pore structure via framework interpenetration or noninterpenetration is capable of triggering the pore confinement effect for the selective capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas molecules from flue and biogas compositions. Another approach in realizing such separations is to make use of OMS with the preferential binding of a targeted gas molecule $\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)$ over others $[\mathsf{N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4})$ . In fact, a large number of MOFs have been demonstrated for a wide range of gas separations by employing the OMS approach, and the library of such MOFs is quite rich in the literature. The metal centers in MOFs are always not saturatively coordinated with the ligands; they are often coordinated with several solvent molecules and thus act as OMSs after thermal activation of the solvent-exchanged or as-made MOF materials. The frameworks having OMSs offer housing of the sieved gas molecules through specific binding interactions (such as coulombic interactions and hemi-coordination bond formations), as the partially positively charged OMSs could act as Lewis acidic centers. These OMSs have a significant impact not only on selective targeted gas sorption but also on triggering high separation selectivity.
Considering this approach, in 2013, Zhou et al. developed a 3D porous Cu-MOF, PCN-88, possessing a high density of open metal sites with a separation distance of $7.4\mathrm{~\check{A}~}$ along the cavity between two opposite Cu OMSs, effective for selectively trapping of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules.[263] The framework possessed an exceptionally high solvent accessible volume of $\bar{13}667.3\mathring{\mathrm{~A}}^{3}$ $73.7\%$ of the total unit cell volume) and exhibited a BET surface area of $3308\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The single component sorption isotherm displayed 5 and 45 times higher targeted $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity $(94\mathrm{cc}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ over competing $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ $(18\ \mathrm{cc\{g}^{-1})}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ $(2.1\mathrm{~cc~g^{-1}})$ molecules, respectively, at ambient temperature (296 K) under 1 atm pressure. Such a high selective capture of targeted $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules could be attributed to the strong host-guest interaction between high-density acidic OMSs and highly polar $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules. At 273 K, IAST separation selectivity calculation exhibited flue $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ $\left(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}15{:}85\right)_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ and biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ (v/v 50:50)] separation selectivity of 23.1 and 7.1, respectively, under 1 atm pressure (Table  3, Sl. No 53). To visualize the preferential binding sites of the captured $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules, a grand canonical Monte Carlo (GCMC) simulation study was performed, which showed that $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules preferentially interacted with the Cu OMSs due to strong electrostatic interactions and resided inside the cavity with optimum fitting (Figure 14a,b), whereas competing gas molecules were located toward the organic units. Furthermore, to verify the separation selectivity triggered through OMSs, an analogous PCN-88’ framework was constructed where no OMSs were present and Cu sites were coordinated and thus blocked by pyrazine molecules (Figure  14c). Similar to the pristine framework, PCN-88’ also possessed almost the same surface area $(3042~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and solvent accessible volume $(72.6\%)$ but exhibited a lower $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity at both temperatures (273 and 296 K), which further led to significantly lower flue and biogas separation selectivity due to the blocking of OMSs. Therefore, this comparative study signified the necessity and effectivity of OMSs toward enhanced flue and biogas separation selectivity and further made the “selectivity triggers through open metal sites $(O M S s)^{\prime\prime}$ approach promising in this direction.
In 2017, Bai et  al. developed a 3D distorted $[\mathrm{Mn}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{4}\mathrm{N}_{2}]$ cluster-based microporous Mn-MOF, NJU-Bai33, which possessed one coordinated DMF solvent molecule per metal center (Figure 15a) with channel dimensions of $4.0\times4.8\mathrm{~\AA~}^{2}$ and a surface area of $884.8~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .[256] Single component $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ sorption isotherms for two temperatures (273 and 298 K)
exhibited significantly higher uptake of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules (86.2 and $65.6\mathrm{cc}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ) over other competing gas molecules $\mathrm{(CH_{4}}$ and ${\mathrm{N}}_{2}^{\prime}$ ), which proved its potential for selective capture of the targeted $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules. The high selective capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules could be attributed to the presence of high-density OMSs throughout the framework, originating after thermal evacuation. As a result, this MOF exhibited high flue $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (v/v 15:85)] and biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ (v/v 50:50)] separation selectivity of 40.3 and 8.9, respectively, at 298 K under 1 bar (Table 3, Sl. No 49, Figure  15b). Although the pore channels are relatively higher to realize selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption, abundant OMSs played a significant role in attaining the enhanced flue and biogas separation selectivity.
Along the same line, in 2017, Wang et  al. constructed a 3D porous MOF, $\mathrm{[Pb}_{2}\mathrm{(L)]}\cdot2\mathrm{DMF}\cdot2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{\ell}}$ , exhibiting two types of nanotubes (cylindrical and triangular prisms) (Figure 16a) with high-density OMSs, potential for efficient $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture.[357] SCXRD structural analysis revealed coordinated water and DMF solvent molecules, which upon activation further led to the formation of OMSs. At 273 and 298 K, the single-component gas sorption study displayed 4 and 5.4 times higher selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture (95.8 and $56.9\ \mathrm{cc\g^{-1}}$ , respectively) than $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ (25.7 and $10.5~\mathrm{cc~g^{-1}}$ , respectively) and further confirmed the efficiency of this MOF toward selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture. IAST selectivity calculation revealed biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ (v/v 50:50)] separation selectivity values of 118.8 and 7 at 273 and $298~\mathrm{K},$ , respectively (Table 4, Sl. No 21, Figure 16b). The high separation selectivity at room temperature could be attributed to the effect of OMSs.
$\mathrm{In}2017\mathrm{\Omega}$ , Qian et al. developed heterocyclic 3D $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF, ZJU-197, consisting of three types of SBUs with channel diameters ranging from 3.7 to $6.8\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ , suitable for effective capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over competing $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\Nu_{2}$ .[191] A single component gas sorption study exhibited significantly lower $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ uptakes than $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (almost 5 and 16 times lower for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ , respectively) even at ambient temperature (298 K) and further made this material effective toward flue and biogas separation. IAST selectivity calculation revealed that ZJU-197 possessed a high flue gas $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (v/v 15:85)] separation selectivity of 514.1 along with moderate biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ $\mathrm{(v/v~50{:}50)}]$ separation selectivity (Table 3, Sl. No 9) at 298 K. The $\varrho_{\mathrm{st}}$ values (isosteric heat of adsorption) at zero coverage displayed a stronger interaction between the framework and linear undersized acidulous $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule $(24.1\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1})}$ than nonpolar $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (11.8 and $11\mathrm{kJ~mol^{-1}}$ , respectively). Such a high separation selectivity and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding affinity could be attributed to the presence of highdensity OMSs acting as Lewis acidic binding sites. This study demonstrated that the construction of heterocyclic MOFs with multiple types of SBUs by employing “selectivity triggers through open metal sites $(O M S s)^{\prime\prime}$ strategy could be an effective approach for the efficient separation of flue and biogas.
## 4.3. Selectivity Triggers Through Surface Functionalization
As discussed in the previous sections, employment of the $1^{\mathrm{st}}$ approach can significantly trigger pore confinement, whereas the $2^{\mathrm{nd}}$ approach is also efficient in binding the sieved gas molecules through specific interactions by offering Lewis acidic sites and hence realizing enhanced flue and biogas separation selectivities. However, proper tuning of surface functionalization is equally important to bind the sieved gas molecules by offering Lewis basic sites. Such an approach will enable selective gas molecule capture and hence obtain high targeted separation selectivity. As guest gas molecules possess a wide range of quadruple moments, dipole moments, and polarizability, the immobilization of dynamic bare functional binding sites onto the pore surface (either hanging from the backbones or residing on the backbone) can furnish specific and/or selective recognition of guest molecules and hence potentially boost the adsorbate-adsorbent interaction. Similar to conventional amine scrubbing, amines can be attached to MOFs by taking advantage of specific amine chemistry, as recently demonstrated by Smit et  al.[459] A functionalized $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ framework was constructed in which $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules could be inserted into metal-amine bonds and thus displayed high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake at low pressure. The uncoordinated amine of the mmen $\langle N,N^{\prime}$ -dimethylethylenediamine) molecule acts as a strong base to remove the acidic proton from the metal-bound amine of a neighboring mmen molecule. Deprotonation occurs only in the presence of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , such that simultaneous nucleophilic addition of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ resulted in the formation of a carbamate with an associated ammonium countercation.[460]
By thorough investigation, we found that a handful of MOFs possessed uncoordinated functional groups (such as $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , – $\mathrm{CF}_{3}$ , ${\mathrm{-CH}}_{3}$ , $-\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ , $-\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ , etc.), which are hanging from the backbone and directed toward the well-defined 1D nanochannels and showed promise for flue and biogas separations. In addition, there are quite a few MOFs where even though no such functional groups hang from the backbone, they possess several uncoordinated heteroatom sites residing on the backbone (such as bare O and N). Such heteroatomic sites are mainly generated due to the use of heterocycle ring-based ligands and further efficiently trigger flue and biogas separation selectivity by offering these basic binding sites for interacting with acidic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules. The presence of such uncoordinated N or O atoms can also facilitate the formation of van der Waals interactions and H-bonding interactions with guest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules rather than $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ , which further leads to higher separation selectivity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and makes the developed MOF material promising for this projected flue and biogas separation domain. All these functional groups, which either hang from the backbones or reside on the backbone, make the MOF surface polar and exhibit efficient capture of selective gas molecules through these Lewis basic binding sites. Additionally, to obtain maximum potentiality through surface functionalization, some pioneer groups designed and developed MOFs with multiple types of surface functionalization together, such as both hanging functional groups and uncoordinated heteroatom sites at a time. Hence, both of these types of surface functionalization can efficiently capture selective gas molecules through their combined effects and will surely be beneficial over single surface functionalization. Observing this for a clear understanding, we have divided this “Selectivity triggers through surface functionalization” section into three parts: a) “Driven by functional groups only,” b) “Driven by bare heteroatoms,” and c) “Driven by multiple functional sites.”
## 4.3.1. Driven by Functional Groups Only
To check the potential of surface-functionalized $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ groups flanked from the backbone toward the pore channels, in 2017, Si et al. developed two robust 3D MOFs, 1 and $\mathsf{1{-N H}}_{2}$ , among which $\mathsf{1}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ not only exhibited a high selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity but also displayed enhanced flue and biogas separation selectivity compared with pristine framework $1.^{[217]}$ SCXRD structural analysis revealed that $\mathsf{1}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ possessed a relatively smaller channel dimension of $8.72\times5.92\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ than $1(9.9\times9.5\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2})$ , attributed to the occupation of pore space to some extent by the flanked uncoordinated $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ groups. Even though $\mathsf{1{-N H}}_{2}$ possessed a relatively reduced BET surface area compared to the pristine one (Table  3, Sl. No 31, 32), it exhibited an enhanced $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity compared with 1 (49.3 and 33.6 at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ under 1 bar for 1 and $\mathsf{1}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , respectively) and proved the efficient preferential binding ability of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ through surface functionalized $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ groups. IAST selectivity tabulation showed that $\mathsf{1}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ exhibited ${\sim}1.7$ times higher flue gas $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (v/v 15:85)] separation selectivity of 104 than 1 (Table  3, Sl. No 31, 32), whereas biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ $\mathrm{(v/v~50{:}50)}\mathrm{\cdot}$ separation selectivity was 12.8 and 8 for $\mathsf{1}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ and 1, respectively, at $298\ \mathrm{K}.$ Such an improved selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity and enhanced separation selectivity could be attributed to a strong dipole−quadrupole interaction between $\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{N}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}_{2}$ functionalization of the host framework and guest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules, which nicely proved the significance of surface functionalization toward this projected application direction. Furthermore, to verify the actual adsorption-based separation performance of the developed materials, a dynamic column breakthrough experiment was performed, which also exhibited a higher retention time of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules for $\mathsf{1}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ than 1, as depicted in Figure $17\mathrm{a-d}$ . The dynamic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity in $\mathsf{1}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ was higher $(0.83\mathrm{~mmol~g^{-1})}$ than that in 1 $(0.41\mathrm{~mmol~g^{-1}})$ at room temperature. The relatively shorter breakthrough time for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ than $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ in $\mathsf{1}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ could be attributed to the relatively small (50:50) separation ratio of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ compared to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ (15:85) (Figure $\boldsymbol{17}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{b}}$ , d). To obtain better insight and the probable position of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules inside the pores, DFT (density functional theory) calculations were performed, which showed the preferential adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules parallel to the plane of acylaminos and the increment of the specific spatial possibility distribution near $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ functionalization (Figure 17e). This study demonstrates that the effective design and development of MOFs with uncoordinated functional $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ groups will be an effective approach to remarkably trigger flue and biogas separation selectivity.
As moisture is considered a troublesome component in coal-fired power plants for flue gas streams (often containing $8\mathrm{-}10\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ ) and $5-10\%$ for biogas, the effective design and development of moisture-stable MOFs is on demand for practical implementations. Considering this, in 2020, our group constructed a 2D moisture-stable microporous $Z\mathrm{n}$ framework, IITKGP-12, possessing ${\approx}4.2\times6.2\mathrm{~}\mathring{\mathrm{A}}{}^{2}$ 1D polar channels decorated with a polar sulfone $(-\mathsf{S O}_{2})$ moiety and displaying excellent flue gas separation selectivity even in moist conditions.[188] The framework displayed excellent robustness through 8 days of $98\%$ RH stability along with 40 days of open-air stability, which could be attributed to the presence of high-density metal$\mathrm{~N~}$ coordination and the absence of OMSs. The higher selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture $(44.8\mathrm{\cc\g^{-1}})$ than $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (20.8 and $7.6~\mathrm{cc~g^{-1}}$ respectively) could be attributed to the presence of surface-functionalized polar sulfone groups throughout the framework. The IAST separation selectivity calculation displayed flue $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ $(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}~15{:}85)]$ and biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ (v/v 50:50)] separation selectivities of 40 and 12, respectively, at $295\mathrm{~K~}$ under 1 bar (Table 3, Sl. No 7). Additionally, to appraise the actual separation performance of IITKGP-12, a dynamic breakthrough study was performed in both dry and wet flue gas mixtures and dry biogas mixtures, as depicted in Figure 18a. The corresponding breakthrough separation timings were 98 and 78 for the dry and wet flue gas mixture, respectively, whereas for the dry biogas mixture, the breakthrough time was $36\mathrm{min}$ with an initial elusion of competing gas molecules ( $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ ) for every case. No significant loss in separation performance up to four successive sorption and desorption cycles further made this developed framework promising in this direction (Figure 18b). Most importantly, the separation performance was largely unaffected even in the presence of the moisture of the simulated flue gas stream, which signified the framework effectivity in this direction. To determine the preferential binding interaction of the captured $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules, GCMC simulation was performed, which displayed a high population of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules neighboring the sulfone moieties and signified strong host-guest interactions due to surface functionalization. Moreover, electropositive C atoms of the guest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules strongly interacted electrostatically with the electronegative O atom of the sulfone moieties with distances of 2.94 and $4.20\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . Thus, this study showed an important direction toward the development of humid-stable MOF potential for flue and biogas separations under both dry and wet environments.
Recently, in 2020, Cui et  al. developed an ultramicroporus Ni-MOF, ZU-36-Ni (GeFSIX-3-Ni), with electronegative $\mathrm{GeF}_{6}{}^{2-}$ anion pillars via mechanochemical (ball milling assisted)
synthesis, which possessed abundant electronegative F-atoms surface functionalization, projected toward well-defined pore channels having a suitable contracted pore diameter of $3.35\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ .[236] The potentially polarized electronegative F atom-decorated pore channels and the contracted pore size in ZU-36-Ni exhibited a high affinity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules with a steep uptake of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at very low pressure (1.07 mmol $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a low pressure range of ${<}400\ \mathrm{ppm})$ ) and proved its potential for the selective capture of targeted $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ guest molecules. The IAST selectivity calculation revealed a flue $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (v/v 15:85)] and biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ $\mathrm{(v/v~50{:}50)}]$ separation selectivity of 4200 and 930 (Table  3, Sl. No 45), which could be attributed to the abundance of electronegative F-atoms for suitable selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption and further validated the efficiency of ZU-36-Ni as a promising adsorbent for real $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ separation. To analyze the actual separation performance, dynamic breakthrough experiments for the flue $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (v/v 15:85)] and biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ (v/v 50:50)] binary mixtures were performed, which displayed initial high purity elution of competing gases $(\Nu_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4})$ with breakthrough retention times of 30 and $10\ \mathrm{min\g^{-1}}$ , respectively, for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , as depicted in Figure 19a. To better understand the host-guest interactions, a DFT simulation study was performed, which showed that the electropositive C atoms of the adsorbed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules were surrounded and efficiently interacted through $\mathrm{F}\cdot\cdot\cdot\mathrm{C}$ interactions (the distance was $3.08{\mathring{\mathrm{A}}})$ with four electronegative F atoms inside the pores, as displayed in Figure  19b. This study demonstrated the efficiency of mechanochemical MOF synthesis while highlighting the superior trapping ability of ZU-36-Ni for targeted $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas molecules with a high separation selectivity due to efficient surface functionalization.
Along the same line, to check the potential of carbonyl and sulfone functionalization toward flue gas separation, Li et  al. developed two UiO-67 analogous 3D $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs, BUT-10 and BUT-11, which possessed two types (octahedral and tetrahedral) of polyhedral cages decorated with carbonyl and sulfone moieties, respectively.[455] At 77 K, BET surface area analysis of BUT-10 and BUT-11 revealed decreased surface areas of 1848 and $1310\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, compared with pristine UiO-67 $(2505\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , which further proved that the surface functionalized groups were flanked toward the interior of the 1D channels. Interestingly, both frameworks displayed more than double the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake amounts (50.6 and $53.5~\mathrm{cc~g^{-1}}$ for BUT-10 and BUT-11, respectively) at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ under 1 atm than pristine UiO-67 $(22.9\mathrm{\cc\{g}^{-1}})$ and proved the efficiency of surface functionalization toward the selective capture of targeted gas molecules. The competing gas molecule $(\Nu_{2})$ uptake at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ was significantly less (2.9 and $2.4\csc\mathrm{{g}^{-1}}$ for BUT-10 and BUT-11, respectively) for both frameworks and made the surface functionalized framework promising for the flue gas separation direction. IAST selectivity calculation at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ revealed approximately 2 and 4 times higher flue gas $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (v/v 15:85)] separation selectivity of 18.6 and 31.5 for BUT-10 and BUT-11, respectively, than pristine UiO-67 (9.4) (Table 4, Sl. No 50–52). The enhancement of separation selectivity could be ascribed to the presence of surface functionalization, which triggered strong host-guest interactions via the electron donor-acceptor effect between the O atoms of the carbonyl or sulfone groups and the C atoms from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules for BUT-10 and BUT-11, respectively. To gain better insight into preferential $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule binding in BUT-11, GCMC simulation was performed, which showed that $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules were captured efficiently around the sulfone groups, as depicted in Figure 20. Additionally, two types of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ interaction modes were observed: (i) the cross interaction mode with distances of 2.96 and 3.07 Å between the C and O atoms of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules and the sulfone group, respectively (Figure  20a), and (ii) the parallel-interaction mode with a distance of $2.92\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , as displayed in Figure  20b. Hence, this study showed the effectivity of surface functionalization for not only efficient selective capture of targeted gas molecules but also demonstrated the necessity of judicious choice of surface functionality to obtain maximum separation potentiality.
To verify the impact on trifluoromethyl functionality $(-\mathrm{CF}_{3})$ toward flue and biogas separation, Chen et al. developed a noninterpenetrated 3D porous MOF, UTSA-121, having randomly dangled ${\mathrm{-CF}}_{3}$ groups projected toward the well-defined 1D rectangular pore channels (Figure 21a).[199] Single crystal structure analysis revealed that UTSA-121 possessed $5.0\ \times\ 6.2\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ and $7.1\times14.5\mathrm{~\AA^{2}}$ channel dimensions along the crystallographic $a$ - and $c$ -axes, respectively (Figure 21b,c), with a BET surface area of $1081\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . A single component $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ gas sorption study revealed that $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake $(59.4\csc\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ was more than five times higher than $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ ( $\mathrm{11~cc~g^{-1}})$ and 15 times higher than $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ uptake $(3.9\mathrm{~cc~g^{-1}})$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ ambient temperature. Such a high uptake difference signified the strong interaction between the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ guest molecules and the trifluoromethyl functionality $(-\mathrm{CF}_{3})$ of the host framework, whereas the less polar nature of $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ resulted in relatively weaker interactions with the polar pore surface and led to significantly lower uptake amounts than $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . IAST selectivity calculation at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1  bar revealed that UTSA-121 possessed selectivity values of 42 and 10 for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ $(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}~15{:}85)$ and equimolar $(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}\ 50{:}50)$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ binary mixtures, respectively (Table 3, Sl. No 16).
Along the same line, to verify the effectivity of nitro $(-\mathsf{N O}_{2})$ groups over bromo $(-\mathrm{Br})$ surface functionality toward this direction, in 2020, the same group developed a 3D microporous nitro-functionalized Cu-MOF, UTSA-93 $(\mathrm{CuBDC}\cdot\mathrm{NO}_{2})$ , having uncoordinated ${\mathrm{-NO}}_{2}$ groups faced toward the interior of pore channels and was isostructural with bromo-functionalized analog, CuBDC-Br.[197] As the size of the $-\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ group is relatively bulkier than the $-\mathrm{Br}$ functionality, the presence of the nitro functionality in UTSA-93 effectively tuned the pore with a smaller size $(6\times7\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ along the $c$ -axis) compared to the prototype bromo-substituent $(6.9\times8.5\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2})$ and efficiently enhanced the selective capture of targeted $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules over others $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}^{\mathbf{\Lambda}}$ ). UTSA-93 captured a negligible amount of other competing gases (0.62  mmol $\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and 0.19  mmol $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{g}^{-1}\mathbf{\Lambda}\mathsf{N}_{2}\mathbf{\Lambda}}\end{array}$ ) at $298{\mathrm{~K~}}$ , which further demonstrated the selective capture of targeted $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules and made UTSA-93 promising toward flue $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (v/v 15:85)] and biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ (v/v 50:50)] separations. IAST selectivity calculation revealed $10-23\%$ enhanced flue and biogas separation selectivity (Table 3, Sl. No 12–13) for UTSA-93 than CuBDC-Br and could be attributed to the compact pore structure in UTSA-93 triggered by nitro functionality compared to CuBDC-Br (Figure 22a,b). To identify the preferential sorption sites of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ guest molecules, a GCMC simulation study was performed, which displayed the confinement of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules inside the pores and bound with the aromatic moieties of the host frameworks through weak electrostatic interactions. Furthermore, to assess the practical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture capability and separation selectivity, a dynamic breakthrough experiment of a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ (v/v 15:85) mixture for UTSA-93 was performed, which exhibited $^{-13}$ min retention of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules with an initial elution of pure grade $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (Figure  22c). In addition to selective sorption capacity and separation selectivity, recyclability and easy regenerability are equally important in terms of overall potentiality. The dynamic breakthrough experiments of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ $(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}~15;85)$ mixture displayed no remarkable loss of separation capability even after multiple cycles (Figure  22d), which further made UTSA-93 promising for flue gas separation.
## 4.3.2. Driven by Bared Heteroatoms
Although the bare functional sites can efficiently trigger flue and biogas separation selectivity, as discussed in the previous section, the presence of bare heteroatom sites is also capable of triggering flue and biogas separation selectivity effectively, as demonstrated by several groups. In 2018, our group developed a 3D microporous $_{Z\mathrm{n-MOF}}$ , IITKGP-7, which possessed rhombus-shaped channels $(5.3\times6.4\mathring\mathrm{A}^{2}$ along the $b$ -axis) decorated with high-density uncoordinated polar heteroatom sites (N and O both).[184] Even though IITKGP-7 displayed a relatively lower BET surface area $(141~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , it exhibited a significant uptake difference between $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ $(29.3{\mathrm{~cc~g}}^{-1})$ ) and other competing gas molecules (13 and 3.6 for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ , respectively) at 295 K. IAST selectivity tabulation displayed a flue gas $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ $\left(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}15{:}85\right)]$ separation selectivity of 121.4 with a biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ $\mathrm{(v/v~}50{:}50)]$ separation selectivity of 8.8 (Table  3, Sl. No 3) at ambient temperature under 1 bar despite having modest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake, which made this material promising for this direction and exhibited the efficiency of high-density uncoordinated polar heteroatom sites.
In the same year, employing the same approach and considering the industrial practicability, our group developed another 3D microporous “highly kinetically stable” Co-MOF, IITKGP-8, which not only exhibited selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity and high separation selectivity but also exhibited excellent moisture stability.[185] Similar to IITKGP-7, IITKGP-8 also exhibited rhombus-shaped channels $(5.4\times6.2\ \mathring\mathrm{A}^{2}$ along the $b$ -axis) decorated with high-density uncoordinated polar heteroatom sites (N and O both) and displayed potential for use in flue and biogas separation. The moisture stability of IITKGP-8 was verified through PXRD analysis coupled with BET surface area analysis at $195\mathrm{~K~}$ . To verify the moisture stability, IITKGP-8 was first exposed to $40\%$ RH for 10 days and displayed excellent structural integrity. For intended applications, understanding the range of moisture stability of the developed MOFs is necessary to expedite proper selection, and the framework stability under higher humidity conditions $90\%$ RH) was also checked for 6 days of exposure. In addition to the PXRD retention ability, the $195\mathrm{~K~}$ BET surface area analysis of IITKGP-8 displayed no significant loss in surface area (224 and $205{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}$ for the pristine and moisture-treated samples, respectively) even after $90\%$ RH exposure for 6 days and confirmed the excellent moisture stability of IITKGP-8 in an extended humidity region (40 to $90\%$ RH). Due to such a high moisture stability, IITKGP-8 lies in the category of “high kinetically stable” MOF, as categorized by Walton et  al.[90,96] Such a high moisture stability could be attributed not only to the presence of stronger Co-N coordination of the paddlewheel SBUs, $\{\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{4}\}$ but also to the absence of OMSs, which are often attacked by moisture and make the developed MOF unstable. The single component $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ gas sorption study revealed selective capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules (55.4 and $26.5~\mathrm{cc~g^{-1}}$ at 273 and $295\mathrm{~K~}$ respectively) over others (9.6 and $3.5\mathrm{cc}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ , respectively, at 295 K) and exhibited flue gas $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ $\mathrm{(v/v~15{:}85)}$ ] separation selectivity of 43.7 and biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ (v/v 50:50)] separation selectivity of 17.1 at ambient temperature (Table  3, Sl. No 4). The selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity and separation selectivity could be attributed to the presence of high-density uncoordinated polar heteroatom sites (N and O), which enhanced Lewis base-Lewis acid interactions efficiently and made this approach viable for flue and biogas separations.
In 2019, Zhang et  al. also developed a 3D microporous Cu-MOF, FJU-44, possessing 1D rhombic channels decorated with uncoordinated $\mathrm{~N~}$ heteroatoms and making the pore surface polar in nature for the selective capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas molecules.[235] At 77 K, $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ sorption revealed a BET surface area of $629.5~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1,}$ and PLATON analysis exhibited a $44.8\%$ solvent accessible volume per unit cell, which proved that FJU-44 was highly porous in nature. A single-component gas sorption study indicated the selective capture of targeted $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas molecules $(53.2\mathrm{~cc~g^{-1}})$ over other competing gases (11.3 and $2.7\ \mathrm{cc\g^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ , respectively) at room temperature (296 K), which could be attributed to the strong host-guest interaction between bare N heteroatom sites and polar guest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules. Due to the large uptake difference between $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and other competing gas molecules, FJU-44 displayed an excellent flue gas $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (v/v 15:85)] separation selectivity of 128, whereas the biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ $\left(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}~50{:}50\right)]$ separation selectivity was 16 at room temperature under 1  bar pressure (Table  3, Sl. No 44), as obtained by IAST selectivity calculation. Furthermore, to assess the feasibility of FJU-44 in the actual $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture process from the postcombustion flue gas mixture, a dynamic breakthrough separation study was performed at $296\ \mathrm{K},$ which displayed a clear separation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ gases with an $^{-11}$ min $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ retention time and proved the potential of FJU-44 toward practicality. Additionally, no remarkable loss in separation potentiality up to three consecutive breakthrough cycles further demonstrated the excellent recyclability and easy regenerablity. Thus, this study indicated the potential of MOF construction with uncoordinated N sites not only for the selective capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules but also for realizing higher separation selectivity and made this “Selectivity triggers through surface functionalization (Driven by bared heteroatoms)” approach effective toward practicality.
## 4.3.3. Driven by Multiple Functional Sites
The previous two sections have exemplified the necessity of either bare surface functionalization or the presence of uncoordinated heteroatom sites in triggering flue and biogas separation selectivity. There are quite a few developed MOFs where both the bare surface functionalization and the uncoordinated heteroatom sites are present simultaneously and trigger these projected separations efficiently; such examples will be discussed under this subsection. To obtain the maximum potentiality through the combined effects of multiple functional sites, in 2017, our group constructed microporous $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF, IITKGP-5, which possessed lozenge-shaped channels $(4.2~\times~5.6~\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ along the $b$ -axis) decorated with both uncoordinated $-\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ functionalization and bare N heteroatom sites simultaneously.[182] Even though IITKGP-5 exhibited a moderate BET surface area $(366~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , it displayed selective capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules (56.4 and $49\thinspace\thinspace\mathrm{cc~g^{-1}}$ at 273 and $295\mathrm{~K~}$ , respectively, under 1  bar) over other competing gas molecules ( $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\bf N}_{2})$ ). The IAST separation selectivity calculation revealed flue $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}15{:}85)]$ gas separation selectivity of 435.5 and 147.8 at 273 and $295~\mathrm{~K~}$ , respectively, whereas the biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ $\left(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}~50{:}50\right)]$ separation selectivity was 151.6 and 23.8 (at 273 and $295\ \mathrm{K},$ respectively) under 1 bar (Table 3, Sl. No 1). Such a high selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity and high flue and biogas separation selectivity could be attributed to the strong $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ affinity toward the polar pore surfaces along with quadrupole–dipole interactions between the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules with abundant uncoordinated N and $-\mathsf{S O}_{2}$ functionalization sites.
As water vapor and/or moisture is omnipresent in flue and biogas separation processes, the developed material needs to be water or moisture stable in terms of practicability. Considering this and employing the same approach, in the same year our group developed another water-stable microporous Co-MOF, IITKGP-6, possessed lozenge-shaped suitable pore channels ${\approx}3.4\times5.0\ \bar{\mathrm{A}^{2}}$ (along the $[1-10]$ direction) decorated with bare $-\mathsf{S O}_{2}$ functionalization and uncoordinated N heteroatom sites together.[183] The hydrostability even after $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ water immersion of IITKGP-6 was confirmed not only by PXRD analysis but also by 77 K BET surface area analysis. Good agreement in the PXRD pattern and no significant loss in BET surface area (279 and $254\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for pristine and water-treated samples, respectively) of the water-treated sample demonstrated the excellent water stability of the framework and made this material promising for practical flue and biogas separation processes. The excellent hydrostability of IITKGP-6 could be attributed to the combined effect of stronger metal–N coordination (Co-N) and the absence of OMSs. The absence of OMSs plays a crucial role in framework stability, as they are often attacked by water molecules, which further makes the developed framework unstable.[96] At $295\ \mathrm{K},$ IAST selectivity calculations revealed a flue gas $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ $(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}~15{:}85)]$ separation selectivity of 42.8 and a biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ $\mathrm{(v/v~50{:}50)}]$ separation selectivity of 5.1 (Table  3, Sl. No 2) and further made this material promising for this direction. Such selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture and high separation selectivity could be attributed to the synergistic effect of quadrupole−quadrupole interactions between $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules and quadrupole-dipole interactions between $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules with high-density uncoordinated N sites and bare $-\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ functionalization, respectively. Hence, both of these studies nicely demonstrated that the construction of MOFs having multiple functional sites together can effectively trigger flue and biogas separation selectivity.
To check the potentiality of sulfone moieties $(-\mathsf{S O}_{2})$ along with uncoordinated oxygen heteroatoms toward this flue and biogas separation direction, in 2020, Mandal et al. constructed two 3D microporous $Z\mathrm{n}$ and Cd MOFs, 1 and 2, respectively, possessing polar sulfone moieties and bare O heteroatoms decorated throughout the pore channels.[220] Both frameworks exhibited selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture (18.8 and $22.6~\mathrm{cc~g^{-1}}$ for 1 and 2, respectively) at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ compared with other competing gas molecules ( $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathbf{N}_{2}^{\prime}$ ). Even though both frameworks captured a modest amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules, $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ uptakes were negligible and further led to high flue and biogas separation selectivity. The selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity could be attributed to strong dipole−quadrupole interactions for both the frameworks between sulfone $(-\mathsf{S O}_{2})$ moieties and guest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules. At $298\mathrm{~K~}$ under 1  bar IAST separation selectivity tabulation revealed flue gas $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (v/v 15:85)] separation selectivity of 73.4 and 46.3 for 1 and 2, respectively, whereas biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ $\mathrm{(v/v~50{:}50)}]$ separation selectivity was 2.3 and 9.5, respectively (Table 3, Sl. No 33–34). Furthermore, to determine the preferential binding sites of the guest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules, a CBMC simulation study was performed, which displayed a strong host-guest interaction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules with the sulfone $(-\mathsf{S O}_{2})$ moieties along with the pyridyl groups/bared
O heteroatoms of the frameworks. $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules strongly interacted with sulfone $(-\mathsf{S O}_{2})$ moieties along with the pyridyl groups from TPOM ligand via $\mathrm{C-H\cdot\cdot\cdotO}$ interactions for framework 1, whereas for framework $2{\ C O}_{2}$ molecules interacted with sulfone $(-\mathsf{S O}_{2})$ moieties along with free O atoms of the TPOM ligand, as depicted in (Figure 23). Hence, this study demonstrated the effectivity of the “Selectivity triggers through surface functionalization (Driven by multiple functional sites)” approach toward flue and biogas separations.
Thus, as discussed in the earlier examples, the functional groups and bare heteroatoms can significantly boost the separation performance, while both are in interaction with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in a single MOF platform. However, in some cases, the presence of the hanging functional group can only finely tune the pore size rather than offering preferential binding sites so that the competing gas molecules become unable to enter due to pore confinement. Additionally, if the hanging functional group is hydrophobic in nature, it may provide an extra feature of ultrahigh framework stability in water and/or moist conditions, which is very necessary and demanding for this projected flue and biogas separation. Considering this, in 2014, Chen et  al. developed a tetrazolate-based 3D microporous Zn-MOF, UTSA-49, with multiple functional sites, such as hydrophobic methyl $\left(\mathrm{-CH}_{3}\right)$ groups and uncoordinated backbone N heteroatoms.[193] Single crystal structural analysis showed two types of well-defined 1D channels having pore apertures of $2.9\mathrm{~\AA~}\times3.6\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and $3.6\mathrm{~\AA~}\times4.0\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , suitable for flue and biogas separations. A single component $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ sorption study showed that $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptake was significantly lower than that of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . The high selective sorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules could be attributed to strong dipole–quadrupole interactions induced by the uncoordinated tetrazolate $\mathrm{~N~}$ heteroatoms of the N-rich aromatic rings and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules. Additionally, the hydrophobic nature of the $\mathrm{-CH}_{3}$ functional groups enhanced the framework stability in a humid environment. IAST selectivity calculation revealed flue gas $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (v/v 15:85)] separation selectivity of 193.7 and 93.5 at 273 and 298 K, respectively, whereas a separation selectivity of 34.8 and 33.7 for equimolar biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ (v/v 50:50)] at 273 and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ respectively, under 1  bar pressure (Table  3, Sl. No 10) were obtained. Such a high selectivity value could be attributed to the combined effect of immobilized bare $\mathrm{~N~}$ heteroatom functional sites and the pore confinement driven by bare ${\mathrm{-CH}}_{3}$ functional groups. Hence, this study demonstrated the effectivity of the presence of multiple functional sites to remarkably trigger flue and biogas separation selectivity.
## 4.4. Selectivity Triggers Through Charged Skeleton
The design and development of MOFs bearing polar functional groups is undoubtedly a good choice, as discussed in the previous two approaches, but sometimes, it is difficult to keep them free onto the pore surface, as they tend to coordinate with the metal center during in situ synthesis. For such cases, an alternate way is to develop frameworks having charged skeletons because such frameworks are also capable of selective trapping of guest molecules via strong electrostatic interactions, which further not only enhance flue and biogas separation selectivity but also cooperatively enhance the framework stability, as exemplified below under this category.
Considering this, Zou et  al. reported a 3D microporous anionic $_{Z\mathrm{n-MOF}}$ , $[\mathrm{Zn_{3}L_{2}(H C O O)_{1.5}}][(\mathrm{CH_{3}})_{2}\mathrm{NH_{2}}]_{1.5}\cdot\mathrm{{xDMF}}$ (1), which possessed two types of interlaced 1D channels with dimensions of $4.0\mathrm{~\AA~}\times4.8\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and $4.2\mathrm{~\AA~}\times8.0$ Å.[350] SCXRD structural analysis revealed that many $\mathrm{DMA}^{+}$ cations were present inside the pore channels (Figure 24a) and interacted with the host framework through H-bonding (Figure 24b), which further not only led to the framework’s overall stability but also enhanced biogas separation selectivity by generating an electrostatic field inside the cavity. The anionic framework, 1, displayed a BET surface area of $569.5\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (as obtained by 77 K $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ sorption) with 23 times higher selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture $(57.5\ \mathrm{cc\g^{-1}})$ than competing $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules $(2.48\ \mathrm{cc\g^{-1}})$ at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and displayed the effectivity of the charged skeleton for such separations. The biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ $\left(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}\ 50{:}50\right)]$ separation selectivity was 1744 under 1 bar at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ as obtained by IAST tabulation (Table 4, Sl. No 20) and could be attributed to the enhancement of host–guest interactions $(\mathrm{DMA}^{+}$ cations with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules) driven by $\mathrm{DMA}^{+}$ cation immobilization. Additionally, the linear $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules interacted strongly with the $\mathrm{DMA}^{+}$ ions and further blocked the intercrossed large channels, which enhanced the selective adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules. However, significantly less uptake of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules indicated low binding affinity toward the charged skeleton and could be attributed to relatively low polarizability (Table  2). Hence, this study proved that anionic frameworks with suitable pore sizes are excellent candidates to efficiently trigger biogas separation selectivity and make the “selectivity triggers through charged skeleton” approach viable.
Along the same line, in 2014, Lu et al. developed a microporous anionic 3D In-MOF, $\begin{array}{r}{(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{6}[\mathrm{In}_{10}(\mathrm{TTCA})_{12}].}\end{array}$ 24DMF $15\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (1), constructed from a $\pi$ -electron-rich tricarboxylate ligand, which exhibited channel dimensions of $8\times8\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ along the $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{c}}$ -axis (Figure 25a) and a BET surface area of $726.8\mathrm{~m}^{2}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ .[207] Single crystal structural analysis revealed that 1D pore channels were fully occupied by disordered $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{+}$ cations and several solvent molecules (DMF and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ . Needless to say, cationic $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{+}$ ions were generated from the decomposition of DMF molecules, which further made the overall framework neutral. A single component $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , ${\bf N}_{2}{\bf{:}}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ gas sorption study revealed that this anionic framework captured a high amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at both the investigated temperatures (105.2 and $69~\mathrm{cc~g^{-1}}$ for $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and $298\ \mathrm{K},$ respectively) with a significantly lower uptake of $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ (4.3 and $18.3\mathrm{cc}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at 298 K). To check the flue and biogas separation selectivity of the charged skeleton, IAST selectivity calculation was performed for both temperatures (273 and 298 K), which exhibited $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ (v/v 15:85) separation selectivity of 221 at 273 K (71 at 298 K) and 24 at 273 K (17 at 298 K) for a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ $(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}\ 50{:}50)$ binary mixture under 1 atm pressure (Table  3, Sl. No 27, Figure  25b). Such a high selective sorption capacity and separation selectivity could be attributed to the generation of specific host-guest interactions driven by the charged skeleton $\mathrm{(Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{+}$ cations) and the higher quadrupole moments of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules $(-1.4\times10^{-39}~\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ . Hence, this study nicely corroborated the effectivity of developing an anionic framework and proved the potentiality of the “selectivity triggers through charged skeleton” approach toward this flue and biogas separation direction.
Recently, MOFs with Li-lined pore surfaces have attracted immense attention for selective gas sorption and separation, as Li atoms present on pore surfaces can significantly trigger specific host-guest interactions.[461] Unfortunately, such Li-MOFs or Li-participating MOFs have been reported sporadically.[462–463] Considering this in 2017, Lu et  al. first developed two porous 3D Li−Zn cluster-based MOFs, 1 and 2, among which 2d (desolvated framework) exhibited strong $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding affinity and displayed high flue and biogas separation selectivity.[216]
Most importantly, both the developed frameworks were anionic in nature, in which the presence of $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{+}$ counterions inside the cavity made the overall framework neutral. SCXRD structural analysis revealed that 1 and 2 possessed channel dimensions of $6.24\times9.63\mathrm{~\AA}^{2}$ and $8.58\times7.63\mathrm{~}\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ , respectively, and the channels were fully occupied by $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{^{+}}$ counterions, dioxane, DMF, and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules for 1 and $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{+}$ counterions, lattice $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , and disordered DMF molecules for 2. Although, both frameworks (1 and 2) were constructed based on heterobimetallic $\mathtt{L i-Z n}$ clusters, after solvent-exchange framework 1 collapsed, which could be attributed to the structural difference in $\mathtt{L i-Z n}$ clusters (bimetallic $[\mathrm{ZnLi}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{3}]$ for 1 and tetranuclear $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\mathrm{Li}_{2}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{6}]$ for 2). Hence, the gas sorption study was performed with framework 2, which exhibited a high specific surface area of $602.5\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ , as analyzed by $195\mathrm{~K~}$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption. The uptake amounts of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ were 16 (for $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ ) and ${\sim}4$ times (for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}^{\cdot}$ ) higher than those of the other competing gases (3.6 and $15.9\csc\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ respectively) at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and signified the potential of $\mathtt{L i-Z n}$ clusters based on the charged skelton of 2. IAST selectivity tabulation of framework 2 revealed a flue gas $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (v/v 15:85)] separation selectivity of $277$ at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ (126 at 298 K) and a biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ (v/v 50:50)] separation selectivity of 35 at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ (26 at 298 K) (Table  3, Sl. No 30). Such a high selective capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and separation selectivity could be attributed to the presence of $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{+}$ in the pore channels and the charged skeleton. Thus, this study unlocked a new avenue for the construction of $\mathtt{L i-Z n}$ clusterbased charged MOFs potential for flue and biogas separation and made the approach “Selectivity triggers through charged skeleton” highly promising.
## 4.5. Selectivity Triggers Through Framework Flexibility (Gate-Opening Behavior)
In addition to structural superiority and constructional variability, framework flexibility is another interesting feature of MOF materials and makes a significant contribution to flue and biogas separation. Flexible MOFs display unique guest-responsive sorption behavior by adjusting (expanding or contracting) their pristine pores through possible interesting structural transformations. This framework transformation occurs at both the molecular level as well as in nonsteady states through several physical and chemical stimuli, including operational pressure, temperature, presence or removal of guest species, solvent mixture and so on, which further efficiently triggers separation selectivity.[464–467] The framework dynamicity can be prominent for some cases, which often displays a gate-opening phenomenon and creates large hysteresis-type sorption behavior, also termed as “breathing effect”. However, due to framework flexibility, MOFs sometimes not only efficiently capture targeted gas molecules but also display a higher sorption capacity toward competing gas molecules, which is further prone to lower separation selectivity. That is why the effective design of flexible MOFs and their involvement in this direction is still a daunting challenge.
In 2016, Zheng et  al. developed a flexible 3D Zn-MOF, $\mathrm{[Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{btec})(\mathrm{btzmb})]_{n}\cdot8n\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (1•S, where S denoted solvent molecules), based on semirigid tetrazole−viologen ligands, which exhibited a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule-induced interesting “breathing effect”.[211] Interestingly, a single component dynamic sorption property with a large hysteresis was observed only for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules at both temperatures (273 and 298 K) (Figure 26a), whereas no such hysteresis was observed for other competing gas molecules $(\Nu_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}^{\cdot}$ ) (Figure  26b). At 273 and $298\ \mathrm{K},$ , the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption isotherm illustrated two-step uncommon sorption behavior with a sharp jump after gate-opening pressures of $54~\mathrm{mmHg}$ for $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and $130\ \mathrm{mmHg}$ for $298\mathrm{~K~}$ , as nicely depicted in Figure 26a. Such a sharp jump in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity after gate-opening pressure could be attributed to the expansion of pore structure after filling the initial pore volumes, which promoted the higher $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity of $122{\mathrm{~cc~g}}^{-1}$ and $98{\mathrm{~cc~g}}^{-1}$ at $760~\mathrm{mmHg}$ pressure and 273 and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ temperature, respectively. The VTPXRD study from 30 to $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ temperature displayed a gradual shift in $2\theta$ values to higher values upon heating, attributed to the decrease in pore size due to shrinkage of unit cells, whereas upon cooling the temperature from 150 to $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the shifted PXRD patterns returned to the initial pattern and signified reversible shrinking-swelling of the flexible framework (Figure 26c). IAST selectivity calculation revealed a high flue gas $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (v/v 15:85)] separation selectivity of 210.4 at $298\ \mathrm{K},$ whereas biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ $\mathrm{(v/v~50:50)}]$ ] separation selectivity was 28.6 at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ under 1  bar pressure (Table  3, Sl. No 28) due to a small uptake of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules $(14\mathrm{~cc~g^{-1}})$ and negligible capture of $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ molecules $(2.1\mathrm{~cc~g^{-1}})$ 1 at ambient temperature. Furthermore, molecular dynamics (ab initio) calculations displayed a total of $10{\thinspace}{\mathrm{CO}}_{2}$ molecules (4 and 6 $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules in type I and type II voids, respectively) per unit cell, as depicted in Figure  26d. Additionally, a molecular dynamics study also revealed that $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules were located inside the well-defined 1D channels and efficiently linked to the bare $\mathrm{~N~}$ atoms of tetrazolate moieties with $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}}\cdot\cdot\cdot\boldsymbol{\mathrm{N}}$ distances of
2.82 and $2.83\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , which was shorter than the summation of the van der Waals radii of N (1.55 Å) and C (1.70 Å). Additionally, several weak $\mathrm{C-H\cdot\cdot\cdotO}$ hydrogen bonds were formed between the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules (carbonyl O atom) and the bipyridinium ( $\alpha{\cdot}\mathrm{H}$ and $\beta\mathrm{\cdotH}$ ) moieties.
Along the same line, in 2013, Sumby et  al. developed a 2D microporous flexible Cu-MOF, $\mathrm{[Cu(bcppm)H}_{2}\mathrm{O}]{\cdot}x S$ , which displayed 2D to 3D structural transformation with a significant reduction of limiting pore diameter $3.6\mathring{\mathrm{A}}(\mathrm{from}6.9\mathring{\mathrm{A}})$ ) (Figure 27) upon activation at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 3 days (termed [Cu(bcppm) $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}]$ -ac).[435] Interestingly, this transformation is irreversible even after resolvation, suggesting that the material was “locked” in the contracted state and selectively captured $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas molecules over $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ through pore confinement. Such a unique structural change from 2D to 3D with decreased pore size could be attributed to the coordination environmental change of the copper(II) metal center from five coordination to a Jahn−Teller distorted octahedral six coordination, as analyzed by the SCXRD study. The activated framework displayed $24\mathrm{~h~}$ water exposure stability due to the narrower hydrophobic pore channel. The surface area analysis possessed a relatively higher surface area $(175~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ for $\mathrm{[Cu(bcppm)H_{2}O]}–a c$ than the pristine one $(155\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and proved the significant framework flexibility. As depicted in Figure $27\mathrm{c}$ , the adsorption and desorption pathways of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture did not fully coincide with each other and displayed small hysteresis, which could be attributed to the small pore aperture in the MOF and further evident the framework flexibility. Despite the modest uptake, $\mathrm{[Cu(bcppm)H_{2}O]}{\cdot}a_{1}$ c displayed a remarkably high flue gas $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (v/v 15:85)] separation selectivity of 590 at $293\mathrm{~K~}$ under 1 atm (Table  4, Sl. No 38) and proved the potentiality along with the necessity of a flexible framework in this direction. Furthermore, to determine the preferential binding interaction of captured $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules inside the framework, an NVT-simulated annealing study was performed, which suggested that $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules weakly interacted with multiple oxygen and hydrogen atoms lining the pore walls (Figure  27d). Hence, this study demonstrated the necessity and effectivity of framework flexibility to trigger flue gas separation selectivity driven by pore size constriction occurrence through a postprocessing mechanism.
In 2021, Sun et  al. developed two isomorphic azole-based highly stable 3D flexible MOFs, M-btz-as (where $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{0}$ , Ni and “as” represented as-synthesized), among which Co-btz-ht (activated framework) displayed higher biogas separation selectivity than Ni-btz-ht.[371] The PXRD analysis of the activated samples displayed shifting of peak positions toward higher $2\theta$ values than the pristine one and demonstrated the occurrence of structural transformation, as displayed in Figure 28a,b. Additionally, shifted Bragg peaks (002) calculation revealed $11.9\%$ $\mid c\mid$ -axis length decreased (15.75 to $13.88\mathrm{~\AA~}$ for Co-btz-ht, whereas Nibtz-ht displayed $5.4\%$ length decrease which indicated Co-btz was more flexible than Ni-btz. Furthermore, to probe the framework flexibility upon solvent effects, PXRD analysis of the methanol- and ethanol-immersed samples was performed, in which methanol-immersed samples (Co-btz-m and Ni-btz-m) displayed a greatly decreased intensity of the (101) peak, whereas the intensity was enhanced for a peak at $\sim9.8^{\circ}$ , as depicted in Figure  28a, b. From Figure  28a, b, it was observed that methanol caused a relatively greater structural change than ethanol and was attributed to the smaller size and greater polarity of methanol. A larger hysteresis loop in Co-btz-ht was observed and could be attributed to framework stretching, whereas Nibtz-ht displayed a type-I isotherm corresponding to a microporous framework. In addition, to verify the frameworks’ dynamic changes, 195 K $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption was performed for the activated Co-btz-m, which displayed gate-opening behavior at very low relative pressure (0.01 atm) with a saturated uptake of $253\mathrm{cc}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 1 atm (Figure 28c, d). The high selective uptake of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ further led to a biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ $\left(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}~50{:}50\right)]$ separation selectivity of 63 and 54 for Co-btz-ht and Ni-btz-ht, respectively, at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ (Table 4, Sl. No 23–24). The higher IAST separation selectivity of Co-btz-ht than Ni-btz-ht could be attributed to the greater framework flexibility of Co-btz-ht and proved the effectivity of the “Selectivity triggers through framework flexibility (gate-opening behavior)” approach toward biogas separation.
Most of the reported flexible MOFs are either 2D layered structures or 3D porous frameworks and display pronounced framework flexibility with guest-responsive gate-opening phenomena. On the other hand, no attention was given to checking the framework dynamicity for polycatenated MOFs until the benchmark work done by Chen et al. in 2019.[423] They have developed two 3D polycatenated MOFs, 1 and 2, which not only displayed a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ responsive gate-opening phenomenon with pronounced hysteresis at room temperature but also exhibited the impact of framework dynamicity upon metal centers. In the PXRD patterns, the disappearance of low-angle peaks and shifting of the high-angle peak of the methanol-exchanged sample indicated the dynamic behavior of the framework. However, a significant change in PXRD was observed for desolvated samples, indicating structural transformation upon desolvation. Even though both frameworks exhibited similar BET surface areas (Table 4, Sl. No 31) at $195\mathrm{~K~CO}_{2}$ sorption, 1a (activated form of 1) exhibited more pronounced hysteretic desorption than 2a at $273\mathrm{KCO}_{2}$ sorption, which could be attributed to the variation in metal centers (Figure 29). At $273~\mathrm{K}.$ , 1a displayed a gate-opening pressure of 0.68  bar with an initial $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake of 1.56  mmol $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ and possessed a saturated uptake of 3.44 mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at 1  bar (Figure 29a). The three consecutive cycles of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption at $273\mathrm{K}$ displayed no remarkable loss in uptake amount and proved the efficiency in recyclability and easy regenerability. Notably, the remarkably broad hysteresis loop in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption indicated that the adsorbed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules strongly interacted with the framework and were not immediately released upon reducing the external pressure, which further proved strong host-guest interactions. Similarly, 2a also displayed a gate-opening pressure of 0.42  bar with an initial $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake of 1.36  mmol $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and displayed a saturated uptake of 2.08 mmol $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at 1 bar and $273\mathrm{~K~}$ (Figure 29b), but the uptake was relatively lower than 1a. Surprisingly, no such gate-opening pressure or hysteresis loop was observed for other competing gas sorption ( $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ ), which proved the selective guest responsive breathing behavior for both frameworks. Furthermore, IAST selectivity calculation displayed biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ (v/v 50:50)] separation selectivity of 35 and 19 at 273 and 298 K under 1 bar, respectively (Table 4, Sl. No 31) for 1a, which further proved the effectivity of framework flexibility in this direction.
To check the potential of heterometallic MOFs (HMOFs) toward the biogas separation direction, in 2020, Wang et  al. constructed a porous 3D HMOF, $\{\mathrm{[PbZn(L)_{2}]}\cdot\mathrm{DMA}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\}_{n}$ (2), which possessed a 1D rectangular channel of $5.8\times6.3\mathrm{~}\mathring{\mathrm{A}}{}^{2}$ (along the [1 7 7] axis) along with unsaturated $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ and Pb(II) OMSs and uncoordinated O heteroatoms.[379] The developed framework not only displayed selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture but also exhibited gate-opening behavior and indicated the dynamic nature of the framework. At $195\mathrm{K}$ , $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption displayed gate-opening pressure at 0.1 atm with an initial uptake of $35.5\mathrm{cc}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , whereas saturated uptake was $128.2\mathrm{cc}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ under 1 atm. The desorption isotherm did not fully coincide with the adsorption isotherm and generated a broad hysteresis, which proved a strong host-guest interaction and indicated that the gas molecules were locked in the skeleton in the low-pressure region. The single component $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ sorption isotherms displayed selective capture of targeted $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules (39.3 and $25.5\mathrm{\cc\g^{-1}})$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ (14.5 and $11.1\mathrm{cc}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ at both 273 and $298~\mathrm{K}_{:}$ , with framework flexibility indication in each case. Furthermore, the IAST selectivity calculations revealed biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ (v/v 50:50)] separation selectivity values of 8.6 and 4.5 at 273 and 298 K (Table 4, Sl. No 25).
## 4.6. Selectivity Triggers Through the Pore Space Partition (PSP) Approach
As mentioned earlier, to obtain the maximum flue and biogas separation selectivity, the optimization of pore size within a suitable range is very challenging and demands enormous effort. In addition to the efficient capture of targeted gas molecules, larger pores can also accommodate higher amounts of competing gas molecules and thus cause poor selectivity values that are unsuitable for this projected application. It is interesting that the usage of relatively larger ligands often leads to framework interpenetration, hence reducing the pore size and enabling the pore confinement effect, as discussed in the first category. However, larger ligands are not always expected to provide framework interpenetration and thus demand the development of a new strategy to finely lower the pore sizes to the desired level. For such cases, the PSP approach could be more effective and needless to say that employing the PSP approach is one and only option to trigger pore confinement successfully for larger channels. In the PSP approach, a suitable auxiliary ligand is needed, which will be inserted into the pore wall via an in situ synthesis methods or postsynthetic modifications (PSM) to act as a partitioning agent and will eventually slice the larger pore size into small pore segments.[468] The basic difference between framework interpenetration and the PSP approach is that one extra auxiliary ligand is required for the PSP approach to slice the larger pore effectively, and it can also be accomplished postsynthetically, whereas both strategies are effective in triggering the pore confinement effect. However, for the PSP approach, the partitioning agent can also be chosen during PSM synthesis by observing the larger channel dimensions; thus, by judicious selection of the partitioning auxiliary ligand, more control of the pore structure can be achieved.
Considering this, in 2016, via incorporation of polynaphthylene (PN) as an auxiliary ligand in the famous pristine MOF-5, Wang et al. constructed $\mathrm{PN}@\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}5$ , in which the micropores of the pristine framework were partitioned to ultramicropores (from ${\sim}1.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ to $\leq0.7\ \mathrm{nm}$ ).[456] 1,2-Diethynylbenzene (DEB) was used as the monomer to afford PN via in situ polymerization (Bergman cyclization followed by subsequent radical polymerization), which slices the pores in MOF-5 and led $\operatorname{PN}@$ MOF-5 for efficient pore confinement, keeping the underlying structure of the pristine framework unaltered (Figure 30a). Due to the partitioning of the pores in $\mathrm{PN}@\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}5$ through the PSP approach, the selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity was not only enhanced two times (38 to 78 cc $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ under 1 bar) but also the flue gas $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ $\left(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}~15{:}85\right)]$ separation selectivity was triggered 23 times (9 to 212) for $\mathrm{PN}@\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}5$ higher than the pristine framework (Table  4, Sl. No 53–54, Figure  30b). At 77 K, BET surface area analysis exhibited more than 2.5 times lower surface area (3200 and $1200\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for MOF-5 and $\operatorname{PN}@$ MOF-5), which further signified that PN polymers were distributed inside the pore channels and partitioned the extended channels into confined compartments. Additionally, a nonlocal DFT (NLDFT) study displayed reduced pore sizes of 0.37 and $0.81\ \mathrm{nm}$ for $\mathrm{PN}@\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}5$ from the maximum of $0.5\mathrm{-}0.6\ \mathrm{nm}$ and signified the effectiveness of using an auxiliary ligand as a partitioning agent. The enhanced $\boldsymbol{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ value for $\mathrm{PN}@\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}5$ at zero loading $(29\mathrm{~kJ~mol^{-1}})$ could be attributed to the strong adsorbate−surface interactions between the guest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules and the $\mathrm{PN}@\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}5$ framework with both sides or ends of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules, stemming from the confined pore size effect and the greater number of exposed PN aromatic surfaces. Interestingly, $\mathrm{PN}@\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}5$ preserved its structural integrity even after exposure to $40\%$ RH for $40\mathrm{~h~}$ , as confirmed by BET surface area analysis, whereas the pristine framework MOF-5 lost its crystallinity completely within $6\textup{h}$ of exposure. Such a high framework stability of $\mathrm{PN}@\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}5$ in humid conditions could be attributed to the inclusion of aromatic PN in MOF-5, which effectively prevented water attack on the coordination bonds. Furthermore, to assess the practical flue gas separation efficiency, dynamic breakthrough experiments were performed under both dry and humid conditions ( $65\%$ RH), in which $\operatorname{PN}@$
MOF-5 displayed negligible loss in separation performance even after the second cycle, whereas MOF-5 exhibited 40 and $73\%$ loss of potentiality in the first and second cycles, respectively (Figure  30c, d). Thus, this study demonstrated the effectivity of the PSP approach not only toward the enhancement of targeted separation selectivity through pore confinement but also to enhance framework stability and make the material viable for practicality.
Along the same line, in 2019, Zhai et  al. constructed six 3D pH stable MOFs, SNNU-51–56, by using C3-tripyridine TPP (2,4,6-tri(4-pyridinyl)-1-pyridine) and TPT (2,4,6-tri(4- pyridinyl)-1,3,5-triazine) ligands as partitioning agents, which efficiently sliced larger hexagonal channels in small triangular channels (Figure 31a).[301] The developed frameworks were partitioned from pristine MIL-88 frameworks and exhibited enhanced targeted $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity with improved separation selectivity through pore confinement, thus demonstrating an important contribution of pore space partition engineering. SCXRD structural analysis revealed that the insertion of TPP and TPT ligands occurred toward open metal sites (OMSs), which further not only dramatically enhanced the targeted gas sorption selectively via pore confinement but also enhanced the $\mathrm{\pH}$ resistivity (3-11) of the frameworks due to the absence of OMSs. PLATON analysis revealed $49\%$ $(3870.3\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3})$ and $47.5\%$ $(3638.5\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3})$ guestaccessible volumes for SNNU-51–52 and SNNU-53–56, respectively. A single-component gas sorption ( $\mathrm{\CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}^{\cdot}$ ) study displayed more than three times higher $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity than competing $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ gas molecules at room temperature (298 K), which further demonstrated the efficient pore confinement guided by PSP. IAST separation selectivity displayed biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ (v/v 50:50)] separation selectivity of 257.8 and 280 for SNNU-51 and SNNU–52 at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ under 1 bar pressure (Table 4, Sl. No 6–11, Figure  31b) and could be attributed to the higher porosity of the framework even after the partition effect than SNNU-53–56. Thus, this study displayed the effectivity of pore confinement through the introduction of a partitioning agent and made “selectivity triggers through the pore space partition (PSP) approach” viable toward this challenging gas separation.
## 4.7. Selectivity Triggers Through Combined Approach
Until now, this review exclusively discusses the developed MOFs based on the approaches taken singly to obtain enhanced flue and biogas separation selectivity. However, there are quite a few developed MOFs where more than one approach has been employed together to obtain the maximum potentiality, which will be elaborately described in this section. Designing and constructing a MOF based on any approach, as discussed in the previous sections, is no doubt a good choice, but obtaining maximum potential combining more than one approach in a single MOF platform will surely lead to high separation selectivity through their combined effect. By a thorough literature survey, we found quite a few developed MOFs, where maximum potentiality was obtained as a combined effect of either OMSs and surface functionalization or OMSs and charged skeleton or surface functionalization and charged skeleton or bare functional sites and pore confinement. Hence, we have divided this section into four subdivisions, e.g., (a) OMSs and surface functionalization, (b) OMSs and charged skeleton, (c) surface functionalization and charged skeleton, and (d) bared functional sites and pore confinement, and discussed comprehensively with suitable examples below.
## 4.7.1. OMSs and surface functionalization
The presence of OMSs can act as Lewis acid binding sites, whereas surface functional moieties can act as Lewis basic sites, and both of them combinedly capture $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules more efficiently than their individual ones, which further leads to higher separation selectivity and makes this combined approach highly promising for flue and biogas separations. Considering this, in 2021, Neogi et al. developed a chemically robust 3D microporous MOF, CSMCRI-9, which possessed porous channels decorated with polar bare surface functionalized hydroxyl (–OH) groups (Figure 32a) combined with high-density OMSs and exhibited selective capture of targeted $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas molecules.[230] The framework displayed excellent robustness not only in several organic solvents for 1 week and in $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ solutions (4-10) for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ but also proved excellent hydrolytic structural integrity even after treatment with pure water for one month and $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ in boiling water. Such a high desirable chemical robustness could be attributed to the presence of ample $\pi\cdot\cdot\cdot\pi$ interactions combined with $\mathrm{C-H}\cdot\cdot\cdot\pi$ stacking interactions throughout the framework governed by the aromatic rings of the ligand and the linker. Even though CSMCRI-9 possessed a moderate BET surface area $(469\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , it selectively captured $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas molecules $(74.83\mathrm{cc}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ which was 3 times higher than $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptake $(25.3\mathrm{~cc~g^{-1}})$ , while $\Nu_{2}$ uptake was negligible. Such selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture is attributed to the strong host-guest $\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)$ interaction between polar surfaces and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules having a higher quadrupole moment than other competing gas molecules (Table 2). Furthermore, the IAST selectivity tabulation at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ displayed flue $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (v/v 15:85)] and biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ (v/v 50:50)] separation selectivity of 43 and 5.39, respectively (Table 3, Sl. No. 40, Figure  32b), which could be attributed to the presence of both surface functionalization (pendent $-\mathrm{OH}$ moieties) and open Cd sites. Interestingly, no significant loss of uptake capacity even after five consecutive $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption (Figure  32c) proved multicycle regenerative $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture and signified the retention of structural integrity. Hence, this study nicely showed the effectivity of the combined approach, i.e., “Selectivity triggers through a combined approach (OMSs and surface functionalization)” to obtain maximum potentiality toward the flue and biogas separation domains.
Along the same line, in 2020, Mandal et  al. developed a chemically stable 3D triazine-based porous Cu-MOF, $\{\mathrm{[Cu(MTABA)(H_{2}O)]}\cdot4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\cdot2\mathrm{EtOH}\cdot\mathrm{DMF}\}_{n}$ (1), having a polar pore surface adorned with high-density OMSs and multiple surface functionalization such as amine, triazine, and methoxy together (Figure 33a).[233] The combined effect of OMSs and polar surface functionalization in 1 not only enhanced the selective capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas molecules over others but also exhibited excellent flue and biogas separation selectivity. The framework displayed not only chemical stability in several protic and aprotic solvents for one day of immersion of the as-synthesized framework but also retained its structural integrity even after 15 days of water exposure and proved the most demanding excellent water stability. The IAST selectivity calculation revealed flue $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (v/v 15:85)] and biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ (v/v 50:50)] separation selectivities of 140 and 9.9, respectively, at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ under $100\mathrm{kPa}$ pressure (Table 3, Sl. No. 42). For long-term continuous potentiality assessment in addition to selective sorption capacity and separation selectivity, high recyclability and easy regenerability are also equally indispensable. Framework 1 exhibited no remarkable loss of selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake cycles (33.6 to $32.4\mathrm{cc}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 298 K), even after five consecutive cycles, proving its excellent recyclability and easy regenerability. The CBMC simulation study (Figure  33b-d) displayed preferential interaction of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules not only with OMSs sites but also with polar amine and triazine N sites through $_{\mathrm{O\cdot\cdot\cdotH-N}}$ $(2.02\mathrm{~\AA})$ and $\mathrm{C-O\cdot\cdot\cdotN}$ $(3.6\mathrm{~\AA~})$ interactions. Interestingly, the number of captured $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules was significantly higher (33) than $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ (4) and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (2) per unit cell at $313\mathrm{~K~}$ under fixed
1  bar pressure and proved the efficiency of the combined approach (OMSs and surface functionalization) toward flue and biogas separation domains via selective capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules.
To display how variation of surface functionality can finely turn limiting pore size for efficient capture of selective gas molecules and excellent separation selectivity can be achieved combinedly assisted through OMSs and surface functionalization, in 2019, Bai et al. developed two isostructural water-stable 3D MOFs, NJU-Bai52 and NJU-Bai53.[259] Both the framework possessed multiple polar functional groups with active binding sites (amide moieties and chloride/monodentate hydroxide) and a high abundance of OMSs exposed to 1D channels together. Although both frameworks exhibited exceptional hydrostability (60 days immersion) and thermal stability (420 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ as obtained by VTPXRD), interestingly, NJU-Bai53 displayed excellent acid-base resistivity $\left(\mathrm{pH2-11}\right.$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ immersion) and kept NJU-Bai53 one hand upper in potentiality than NJU-Bai52. The strong $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding affinity in NJU-Bai53 was not only due to the combined effect of polar functional sites (amide moieties and monodentate hydroxide) along with the high abundance of OMSs but also due to the slightly lower limiting pore apertures of NJU-Bai53 (3.4 Å) than NJU-Bai52 (3.7 Å), suitable for more efficient pore confinement. The observed flue and biogas IAST separation selectivity was 581 and 13.5 for NJU-Bai52 at 298 K under 1  bar (Table  3, Sl. No. 52). Notably, even though the flue and biogas IAST separation selectivity was not tabulated for NJU-Bai53, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ $(0.4/800)$ separation selectivity was calculated from the $S=(q_{1}/q_{2})/(p_{1}/p_{2})$ equation, and the value was 6397 at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ , which was also significantly higher than the selectivity value of NJU-Bai52 (147) obtained from a similar calculation. To elucidate real $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ separation performances, a dynamic breakthrough experiment was performed and exhibited higher breakthrough times for NJU-Bai53 (77 and $36~\mathrm{min}$ , respectively) in flue and biogas separations than NJU-Bai52 (70 and $23\mathrm{min}$ , respectively) (Figure 34a,b) and made NJU-Bai53 promising over NJU-Bai52. At $298\mathrm{~K~}$ recycling breakthrough tests further demonstrated the stable adsorption and separation abilities of the developed frameworks, as depicted in Figure $34\mathrm{c}$ . Furthermore, the GCMC simulation study displayed the preferential locations of guest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules (Figure  34d-f) toward the OMSs and polar functional sites (amide moieties and chloride/monodentate hydroxide) through noncovalent interactions for both frameworks.
The difference in accessibility of uncoordinated N heteroatom functional sites on the developed MOFs with almost porosity may also lead to significant variance in separation selectivity, as exemplified by He et al.[202] Three isostructural NbO-type 3D Cu-MOFs, ZJNU-43, ZJNU-44, and ZJNU-45, were constructed that possessed both OMSs and uncoordinated N heteroatom functional sites exposed toward the pore channels but had different orientations and numbers. As displayed in Figure 35a, ZJNU-43 and ZJNU-44 bear one uncoordinated N atom each at different positions ( $\alpha$ and $\beta,$ respectively), whereas ZJNU-45 has two uncoordinated N atoms at both $\alpha$ positions. The relatively high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ saturated uptake capacity for ZJNU-44 at $296\mathrm{~K~}$ could be attributed to the higher accessibility of uncoordinated N atoms in ZJNU-44 than others, whereas N atoms in ZJNU-43 and ZJNU-45 were partially blocked due to the more steric hindrance effect, as they were present neighboring isophthalate moieties, i.e., $\alpha$ positions. The IAST separation selectivity and transient breakthrough simulations proved the better flue and biogas separation selectivity for ZJNU-44 than others, as tabulated in Table 3, Sl. No 21–23. The transient breakthrough simulation study also implied higher $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ productivity for ZJNU-44 with a higher breakthrough time and high purity $(99.95\%+)$ than ZJNU-43 and ZJNU-45, which further demonstrated the viability of functional site accessibility to obtain maximum potentiality. The comprehensive DFT simulation study (Figure 35b) showed that although the host-guest $\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)$ binding energies $(E_{\mathrm{b}})$ for all three developed MOFs were almost the same in both the triangular window sites and OMSs (denoted in figure as sites A and B, respectively), the calculated binding energy for ZJNU-44 toward uncoordinated N sites (denoted in figure as site C) was significantly higher $(-27.6\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1})}$ than ZJNU-43 and ZJNU-45 (-18.1 and $18.7\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ , respectively, Figure 35b), which further triggered the higher selective capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ guest molecules and made marked differences with others. The high binding energy in ZJNU-44 evident that compared to the $\alpha$ -position $\mathrm{~N~}$ binding sites, open $\beta\mathrm{.}$ position sites prevailed on $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption and proved the necessity of accessible uncoordinated surface functionality (N atoms) at the appropriate positions to enhance preferential $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity and separation selectivity over others.
## 4.7.2. OMSs and Charged Skeleton
As mentioned earlier, unsaturated metal centers can act as Lewis acidic binding sites, and charged frameworks are also capable of selectively binding targeted gas molecules through electrostatic interactions; thus, both of them can combinedly provide maximum flue and biogas separation selectivity, as exemplified under this subcategory. In 2015, using an asymmetrical pentacarboxylate ligand, Wang et  al. developed 3D microporous MOFs, 1·DMF and 1·DMA, with anionic skeletons that possessed two unique types of dinuclear SBUs, $\mathsf{\Gamma}[\mathsf{Z n}_{2}(\mathsf{C O O})_{5}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})]$ .[215] 1·DMF and 1·DMA represent unique examples of the same framework guided by different building units, which are very rare. SCXRD structural analysis of 1·DMF revealed two different shapes of 1D pore channels along the $a$ - axis with channel dimensions of $6.0\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}\times6.4\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ and $4.8\mathrm{~\AA~}\overset{\circ}{\times}11.7\mathrm{~\AA~}$ respectively. The channels of 1·DMF were fully occupied by several solvent molecules (DMF and water) and $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{+}$ counterions. The desolvated framework of 1·DMF (1a) exhibited a BET surface area of $544~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and displayed its potentiality in flue and biogas separations by capturing high amounts of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ guest molecules (85.5 and $60.1\mathrm{cc}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 273 and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ , respectively, under 1  bar pressure). 1a displayed significantly lower uptake of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\Nu_{2}$ at ambient temperature $\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\left(14.8\mathrm{\cc\}\mathtt{g}^{-1}\aftergroup\egroup\right.$ for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $2.3\ \mathrm{cc\}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ at 298 K). To verify the separation selectivity, IAST calculations were performed, which exhibited a selectivity value of 12 (at 298 K) for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ $(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}~50{:}50)$ binary mixtures and 107 (at 298 K) for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (v/v 15:85) mixtures at 1  bar pressure (Table  3, Sl. No 29, Figure 36). Such a high selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity and separation selectivity could be attributed to the highly polar pore surfaces decorated with OMSs and the charged skeleton, which further made this combined approach viable to obtain maximum potentiality.
Similarly, in 2015, Bu et  al. constructed two 3D microporous $Z\mathrm{n}$ and Cd MOFs, PMOF 1 and PMOF 2, among which PMOF 1 possessed both OMSs and $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{^{+}}$ counterions and displayed maximum flue and biogas separation selectivity over PMOF 2.[266] A single component gas sorption study revealed a more than three times higher selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity $(99\mathrm{~c}\mathrm{{c}\ g^{-1})}$ than that of competing $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules $(32\mathrm{~cc~g^{-1}})$ , whereas the uptake capacity was twelve times higher than that of $\Nu_{2}$ $(8\mathrm{cc}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . The high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity could be attributed to the stronger binding interactions with the dual active sites (OMSs and dimethylamine cations) due to the higher quadrupole moment of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules (Table 2). IAST separation selectivity at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ displayed flue $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (v/v 15:85)] and biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ (v/v 50:50)] separation selectivity of 30 and 6.7 under 1 bar (Table 3, Sl. No 55), which further signified the efficiency of the combined approach (OMSs and charged skeleton) employment in this direction.
## 4.7.3. Surface functionalization and charged skeleton
To obtain the maximum separation potential by employing the combined approach (surface functionalization and charged skeleton), in 2016, Du et  al. constructed a microporous moisture-stable charged 3D Zn-MOF, $\{(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bpy}\mathrm{db})_{2}(\mathrm{ATZ})]$ (DMA) $({\mathrm{NMF}})_{2}\}_{n}$ (1), featuring bare amino functionality along with uncoordinated N-donor sites projected toward the pore channels as selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ guest binding sites.[275] The excellent moisture stability $(40-60\%$ RH) even after one month could be ascribed to the employment of a tetrazole-based linker combined with a high $\mathtt{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ -based carboxylate ligand. The framework exhibited selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture over other competing gas molecules $\mathrm{\vertCH_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ ) and displayed the effectivity of preferential binding of targeted gas molecules through surface functionalization and charged skeleton combined. Taking advantage of the large uptake difference at $298\ \mathrm{K},$ flue $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/$ $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ $\left(\mathrm{v/v}~15{:}85\right)]$ and biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ (v/v 50:50)] separation selectivity was tabulated via IAST calculation, which displayed separation selectivity of 127 and 131, respectively (Figure $37\mathrm{a}$ , Table 3, Sl. No 58). Such a high separation performance could be ascribed to the combined effect of bare N-donor sites along with uncoordinated $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ groups and the presence of dimethyl amine counter cations in cavities. A GCMC simulation study revealed that $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules were located near not only the amino functionalities of the linker but also toward the dimethylamine counter cations. The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules interacted with the bare N-donor sites and uncoordinated $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ groups through several van der Waals interactions, with the bpydb2− ligand via $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions, and with dimethylamine counter cations through H-bonding interactions (Figure  37b, c). Interestingly, no remarkable degradation in framework stability or sorption capacity could be observed even after three cycles of the moisture-treated sample, which further confirmed the excellent moisture stability, recyclability and easy regenerability of the framework (Figure 37d).
Along the same line, in 2019, Neogi et al. constructed a chemically robust cationic 3D microporous Ni-MOF, CSMCRI-3, which possessed a polar pore surface decorated with ample uncoordinated basic $\mathrm{~N~}$ heteroatoms and hydroxyl groups.[229] CSMCRI-3 displayed excellent structural robustness in various organic solvents, a wide range of $\mathrm{\pH}$ values (5-10) for 1 day, open air for 1 month, water and $70\%$ RH humidity for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ as confirmed by PXRD analysis. Such chemical robustness and excellent hydrostability are indeed necessary, as practical flue and biogas separation often contains water vapor/moisture. CSMCRI-3 captured a modest amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at $273~\mathrm{~K~}$ at 1  bar $(54.32\mathrm{~cc~g^{-1}})$ ; however, the uptake amount of this targeted gas was significantly higher than that of the competing gases ( $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}^{\prime}$ ), proving its potential for selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture. No significant loss in uptake amounts of the framework after multicycle $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption (Figure 38a) proved the framework recyclability and easy regenerability, whereas negligible loss in uptake amount of $70\%$ RH exposed sample evident the structural robustness and excellent moisture stability. IAST separation selectivity calculation at 273 K under 1 bar revealed high flue $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (v/v 15:85)] and biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}(v/v~50{:}50)]}$ separation selectivity of 128 and 11.7, respectively, as tabulated in Table  3 (Sl. No 39, Figure 38b). The selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity and separation selectivity attributed to the polar pore surface were jointly driven by surface functionalization (bare basic $\mathrm{~N~}$ atoms and hydroxyl groups) and the cationic charged skeleton.
## 4.7.4. Bared Functional Sites and Pore Confinement
The presence of abundant uncoordinated heteroatom sites produces stronger host-guest interactions with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules via induced dispersion and electrostatic forces, whereas a suitable pore size can trigger the pore confinement effect. Hence, combining both approaches is no doubt a good strategy to enhance flue and biogas separation selectivity. Taking advantage of this, in 2015, Lan et al. constructed an air-stable 3D Zn-MOF, NENU520, which possessed a high density of bare polar N-donor sites from tetrazolate moieties projected toward the inner walls of 1D channels with a limiting pore size of $3.6\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ suitable for efficient $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture.[251] NENU-520 displayed IAST selectivity of 400 for flue $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (v/v 15:85)] gas separation and 12.8 for biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ (v/v 50:50)] separation at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ under 1  bar (Table  3, Sl. No 47). The high separation selectivity could be attributed to three factors: (i) the presence of abundant bare N-heteroatom sites projected toward the inner surface of the narrow cavities, (ii) the preferential accessible N-site binding of $C O_{2}$ molecules having greater polarizability along with a larger quadrupole moment than other competing gas molecules, and (iii) the limiting pore size of $3.6\mathrm{\AA}$ of NENU-520, which was just right only for $C O_{2}$ molecules capture, as the kinetic diameters of $\mathrm{{^{CH_{4}}}}$ and $N_{2}$ are higher (3.64 Å and $3.80\mathrm{~\AA~},$ , respectively) than $C O_{2}$ (3.3 Å). NENU-520 exhibited good air stability even after more than two weeks of exposure to air, as confirmed by PXRD analysis. Moreover, no significant loss even after three adsorption-desorption cycles at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ proved the excellent recyclability of NENU-520, whereas the moderate $\boldsymbol{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ value $(33.2\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}})$ of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ signified the low energy-consumption regenerability and further made this material promising. Additionally, to verify the feasibility of the separation performance, a transient breakthrough simulation study was performed, which exhibited a longer breakthrough time for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture with an initial elution of competing gas molecules $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\bf N}_{2})$ (Figure 39). However, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ (v/v 15:85) simulated breakthrough separation exhibited a higher retention of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and high $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ productivity of $4.84~\mathrm{mol~kg^{-1}}$ with a purity of more than $99.95\%$ , which further demonstrated the excellent selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity and flue gas separation selectivity of NENU-520. In terms of practicality, such a longer breakthrough time with high purity is highly desirable to avoid the frequent requirement of column regeneration. This work demonstrated the efficiency of introducing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -philic functionalities, i.e., uncoordinated heteroatom sites along with the effective design of a suitable pore size for pore confinement, which can lead to the trade-off between high sorption capacity with excellent selectivity and make this combined approach viable for the direction of effective flue and biogas separation.
A 3D microporous $(3.4\mathrm{-}4.3\mathrm{~\AA~})$ Zn-MOF, FJI-H29, exhibited selective capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules through pore confinement over other competing gas molecules ( $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ ).[234] The material exhibited high water and acid-base resistivity (from $\mathrm{pH}2\mathrm{-}13)$ ) because of stronger Zn-N coordination. Such a high level of robustness is desirable as water vapor and acidic gas impurities are omnipresent in flue gas and raw biogas. FJI-H29 exhibited remarkably high IAST flue $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ $\left(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}~15{:}85\right)]$ and biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ $(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}~50{:}50)$ ] separation selectivity of 146 and 25, respectively, at ambient temperature under 1  bar pressure (Table  3, Sl. No 43). A dynamic breakthrough study also revealed a higher retention time for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules $(114.2\ \mathrm{min})$ in a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ mixture and $21.2\ \mathrm{min}$ for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ (Figure 40a,b), which further proved the practical separation selectivity and the excellent potentiality of FJI-H29. In addition to having a high sorption capacity and separation selectivity, regenerability and recyclability are equally important in terms of overall potentiality. Interestingly, FJI-H29 displayed a moderate $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption enthalpy of $27.5~\mathrm{kJ~mol^{-1}}$ , which is suitable for a small amount of required regeneration energy, whereas $100\%$ retention of sorption capacity even after five consecutive cycles (Figure 40c) proved the excellent recyclability and made this material promising for this direction. The simulation study revealed that the adsorbed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules resided in the limiting pore (3.4 Å) center, along with three preferential $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption sites, as depicted in Figure  40d-f. It was found that the adsorbed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules weakly interacted with the active H-atoms of triazole and phenyl moieties (Site-I and II) through ${\mathrm{N}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{H}}\cdot\cdot\cdot{\mathrm{O}}$ and $\mathrm{C}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}\cdot\cdot\cdot\mathrm{O}$ bonds (Figure  40d, e) and through $\pi-\pi$ interactions with phenyl rings (Site-III) (Figure  40f). Thus, bare functional sites and pore confinement are responsible for bringing such a high level of flue and biogas separation performance.
## 4.8. Selectivity Triggers Through Composite Formations
The construction of MOF composites through the controllable integration of functional materials with MOFs is undoubtedly a leading approach as well as developing interdisciplinary research areas toward the creation of new multifunctional composites/hybrids. MOF composites exhibit superior properties with respect to their individual integrating components through the combined effect of both functional units into the hybrid system. Despite having several superior properties, MOFs exhibit a few weak points, especially poor chemical stability in many cases, which impede their use in realistic applications. For such cases, composite formation is an alternate way not only to enhance chemical stability but also to trigger their potentiality by introducing new functionalities. To date, several MOF composites have been successfully synthesized by incorporating graphene oxide (GO), metal nanoparticles/nanorods (NPs/NRs), carbon nanotubes (CNTs), ionic liquids (ILs), quantum dots (QDs), polyoxometalates (POMs), biomolecules, polymers, and so on, which further results in outstanding performance unattainable by the individual constituents and signifies the necessity of composite formations.[469]
Considering this, in 2015, Xiang et al. developed UTSA-16–GO composites and checked their potentiality for biogas separation.[292] Depending upon different amounts of GO (9.5, 19 and $28.5~\mathrm{\mg}$ ) incorporation, three variations of composites (UTSA-16–GO9.5, UTSA-16–GO19, UTSA-16–GO28.5) were constructed (Figure 41a), among which UTSA-16–GO19 displayed the maximum potentiality over the other variants. BET surface area analysis revealed that the maximum BET surface area was $529\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for UTSA-16–GO19 and 325 and 487 $\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for UTSA-16–GO9.5 and UTSA-16–GO28.5, respectively; however, the BET surface area was lower than that of the parent UTSA-16 $(628~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ (Figure  41b), signifying successful composite formation. The type I isotherms in all the cases indicated the predominantly microporous nature of the composites. Even though, at $296~\mathrm{K}.$ , single component $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ gas sorption of UTSA-16–GO19 displayed a relatively lower uptake amount than the pristine framework, IAST separation selectivity exhibited three times higher biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ (v/v 50:50)] separation selectivity for UTSA-16–GO19 (114.4) than UTSA-16 (Table  4, Sl. No 1, Figure  41c). The higher separation selectivity of the composite framework than the pristine framework could be attributed to the GO coverage on the core–shell composites and displayed the effectivity of composite formation. Although UTSA-16 is a promising material for efficient carbon capture with low adsorption enthalpy, its industrial use is limited due to relatively low $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ separation selectivity (29.8) and poor thermal stability (framework collapsed at $139^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . However, an enhanced thermal stability (up to $340~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) along with significant selectivity enhancement was observed due to UTSA-16–GO composite formations, which further proved the necessity and effectivity of the composite formation. The efficient composite formation could be attributed to the interactions between the surface O atoms of the carboxylate units of UTSA-16 and the plentiful carboxyl and hydroxyl groups of GO. Moreover, the $\boldsymbol{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ study revealed a significantly higher $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding affinity of UTSA-16–GO19 $(42.1\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1})}$ than the pristine framework $(11.7\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ , which further made the “selectivity triggers through composite formations” approach effective in this direction.
Along the same line, in 2017, Xia et al. constructed a MOF$505@\mathrm{GO}$ composite, which not only displayed enhanced sorption capacity with separation selectivity but also exhibited excellent moisture stability $80\%$ RH) through composite formation.[283] By varying the GO wt% (2, 5, 8, 10) incorporation, several $\mathsf{M O F-}505@\mathsf{G O}$ composites were constructed, among which MOF-505@5GO displayed maximum potentiality toward this direction. It was observed that $\mathsf{M O F-}505@\mathsf{G O}$ composites possessed higher micropore volumes than the pristine MOF-505, which could be attributed to the creation of new micropores at the copper dimers of MOF-505 units and the
GO layers (oxygen-containing functional groups) interface region, as displayed in Figure 42a. Due to composite formation, the selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity was enhanced $33.7\%$ for $\mathrm{MOF}{-}505@5\mathrm{GO}$ from 2.87 to 3.94  mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and $100~\mathrm{{kPa}}$ . Such a high sorption affinity of targeted $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules for $\mathsf{M O F-}505@5\mathsf{G O}$ could be ascribed to the increase in surface area along with the increase in surface dispersive forces. IAST separation selectivity tabulation displayed $33.8\%$ and $13.2\%$ enhancement of flue $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ $(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}~15{:}85)]$ and biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ $(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}\quad50{:}50)]$ separation selectivity compared to the pristine framework (Figure  42b), as listed in Table  3 (Sl. No 61–62) and proved the efficiency of composite formations. The enhanced separation selectivity in $\mathrm{MOF}{-}505@5\mathrm{GO}$ could be attributed to the enhanced dispersive forces along with stronger electrostatic interactions of the GO composite surfaces toward targeted $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules. Additionally, $\mathsf{M O F-}505@5\mathrm{GO}$ retained its structural integrity even after exposure to $80\%$ RH for 15 days, as verified by PXRD analysis. Moreover, the lack of a significant reduction in surface area even after 10 months of exposure to the atmosphere further proved the framework robustness of the $\mathrm{MOF}{-}505@5\mathrm{GO}$ composite. Thus, the excellent stability and enhanced separation selectivity made the $\mathrm{MOF}{-}505@5\mathrm{GO}$ composite promising for use in flue and biogas separation.
Along the same line, to verify the potentiality of encapsulating Ni nanoparticles (NPs) MOF composites for flue and biogas separation, in 2019, Neogi et  al. constructed $\mathrm{Ni}@$ $2\mathrm{rOF}$ , which displayed a $35\%$ enhancement in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity compared to the pristine framework with a high flue gas separation selectivity.[277] The reduced BET surface area of $898~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for the $\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{ZrOF}$ composite compared to the pristine framework $(992\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ could be attributed to the effective encapsulation of ultrasmall Ni nanoparticles inside the modified framework. Although the surface area decreased slightly after composite formation, the selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity was remarkably enhanced by $35\%$ (from 55.6 to $93.3\mathrm{~cc~g^{-1}})$ at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ , which could be attributed to the stronger binding affinity between the polarizable guest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules and the Ni NPs’ auxiliary adsorption sites and proved the effectivity of composite formation. The $\boldsymbol{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ analysis also revealed $10\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ enhancement of adsorption enthalpy and validated preferential interactions between guest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules and NPs. Interestingly, the competing gas molecule $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptake at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ was significantly lower $(23.4\mathrm{~cc~g^{-1}})$ , whereas $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ uptake was negligible, which further led to higher separation selectivity. To verify the sorption recurrence, 10 consecutive cycles of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption study for the $\mathrm{Ni}@Z\mathrm{rOF}$ composite at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ were tested, which displayed no remarkable loss in sorption capacity and highlighted the framework robustness during the cycling process. IAST selectivity calculation displayed a flue gas $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (v/v 15:85)] separation selectivity of 145.7, whereas biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ (v/v 50:50)] separation selectivity was 12.65 at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ under 1 bar (Table 3, Sl. No 59). The high separation selectivity signified stronger electrostatic interactions between the diverse polar functional sites of the $\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{ZrOF}$ composite and guest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules.
## 5. Elucidation of the Sorption Mechanism Through in situ Experimental Studies
In addition to the effective design and development of MOFs for efficient flue and biogas separations, elucidating their sorption mechanism at the molecular level is undoubtedly necessary to obtain deeper insight into the phenomena and thus to further develop the area by realizing the fundamental chemistries involved therein. As gas sorption and separation of MOF is solely dependent upon the structural designability and architectural variability, careful observation throughout the gas sorption process can only provide a better understanding regarding the sorption variability for different gases despite having an unaltered framework. For this purpose, some in situ experiments, such as in situ PXRD, SCXRD, FT-IR and/or neutron/synchrotron studies, have been applied. It is quite inspirational that, in many cases, simulation/computational study has been performed accompanied with experimental study, as exemplified before, which is helpful to identify the preferential binding interactions of the gas molecules. However, to visualize each and every step thoroughly during the gas sorption process, in situ experiments are indispensable. Thus, to gain better insight and further developments, experimental flue/biogas separation studies accompanied by in situ experiments and simulation/computational studies in one system will provide a great benefit to interpret the structure-property relationship.
Considering this and employing the “Selectivity triggers through surface functionalization (Driven by bared heteroatoms)” approach, in 2019, Huang et  al. developed an ultrastable imidazole-based $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF, MAF-stu-1 $[Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{imPim})]$ , which possessed uncoordinated imidazolyl-N polar sites with an effective pore aperture of $4-5\mathrm{~\AA~}$ to accommodate selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules in a fit like-a-glove fashion.[452] The existence of periodic binding pockets with bare polar heteroatomic sites facilitated the selective capture of targeted $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules and thus remarkably enhanced the flue gas $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (v/v 15:85)] separation selectivity, which was clearly visualized through in situ SCXRD structural determination. MAF-stu-1 displayed excellent framework robustness not only in several organic solvents or in water but also exhibited excellent structural integrity even after treatment with boiling water for 9 days and a wide range of $\mathrm{\pH}$ values (2-13). Room temperature $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption displayed a high amount of saturated uptake $(80\mathrm{~cc~g^{-1}})$ with an initial steep uptake even below 150  mbar, which proved the strong $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding affinity of MAF-stu-1. To demonstrate that $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture could be achieved in an ambient environment, the activated MAF-stu-1 was exposed to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ for 5 days, and then in situ SCXRD structural analysis was performed for the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ loaded sample. Interestingly, in situ SCXRD structural analysis revealed that 18 $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules were captured per unit cell with a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ site occupancy of 0.6 and implied the molecular formula $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{imPim})\cdot0.6\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (Figure 43a). The guest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules were efficiently located to one crystallographic independent position within one pocket not only through a number of weak $\pi\cdot\cdot\cdot\pi$ and ${\mathrm{C-H}}\cdot\cdot\cdot{\mathrm{O}}$ interactions but also with exposed imidazolyl-N Lewis basic sites having an $\mathrm{N}\cdot\cdot\cdot\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ separation distance of $4.2\mathrm{-}5.7\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . The IAST separation selectivity at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ revealed a flue gas $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (v/v 15:85)] separation selectivity of 106 (Figure  43b), as tabulated in Table  4 (Sl. No 48). Additionally, the GCMC simulation study (Figure  43c) supported the experimental result and displayed an overall binding energy of $71.69\mathrm{~kJ~mol^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{imPim})\cdot\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , which was the summation of host-guest $\mathrm{(pocket-CO_{2}}$ interaction) and guest-guest $\scriptstyle\left(\mathbf{CO}_{2}-\mathbf{CO}_{2}\right)$ interactions (70.80 and $1.04\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ , respectively). To judge the real-time separation potentiality, breakthrough experiments were performed, which displayed approximately $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ retention of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules in a packed bed with an initial high-purity elusion of $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ gas molecules, as displayed in Figure  43d. Besides selective capture of targeted gas molecules with high separation selectivity, recyclability and easy regenerability are equally important in terms of overall potentiality. Five consecutive breakthrough runs with no remarkable potential loss (Figure  43d) further proved the potentiality of MAF-stu-1 toward this flue gas separation direction. Hence, this study demonstrated the importance of in situ experiments with a partnership of experimental and simulation studies to visualize the preferential binding sites and efficient binding interactions at the molecular level.
Recently, in 2019, employing the “Selectivity triggers through surface functionalization (Driven by multiple functional sites)” approach, Chen et  al. developed UTSA-120, an isoreticular MOF of SIFSIX-2-Cu-i, with dual surface functionalities $(\mathrm{SiF}_{6}^{2-}$ groups and bare N sites on tetrazine rings), which favored the optimal packing of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules into the pore channels.[198] The isoreticular MOF UTSA-120 was synthesized by introducing a secondary tetrazine functionality in a dpa $(4,4^{\prime}$ -dipyridylacetylene) ligand (Figure 44a), which not only enhanced the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity (3.56 mmol $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 296 K) even at low pressure (0.15 bar) but also significantly triggered $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ separation selectivity despite having a similar pore size $({\sim}4.6\mathrm{~\AA})$ . Even though the presence of bare $\mathrm{~N~}$ sites on tetrazine rings remarkably enhanced the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity at low pressure, $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ sorption remained almost unchanged, which made this material promising for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ separation. The IAST selectivity calculations revealed that UTSA-120 possessed almost four times higher $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ (v/v 15:85) separation selectivity at $296\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar (Table 3, Sl. No 14), whereas equimolar $(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}~50{:}50)$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ selectivity was also three times higher than SIFSIX-2-Cu-i and proved the effectivity of the surface functionalization approach. To determine the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding sites and to gain better insight into the host-guest interaction, in situ neutron powder diffraction study was performed. The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -loaded UTSA-120a sample exhibited that the adsorbed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules not only interacted with the $\mathrm{SiF}_{6}{}^{2-}$ anion via C···F bonding, such as SIFSIX-2-Cu-i but also interacted with tetrazine ring bare $\mathrm{~N~}$ sites through $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}}\cdot\cdot\cdot\boldsymbol{\mathrm{N}}$ bonding (Figure 44b-d), further signifying the importance of dual surface functionalities. To check the practical separation ability, transient breakthrough simulations and experimental breakthrough studies were performed for the binary mixture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (v/v 15:85) and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ $(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}\ 50{:}50)$ . The transient breakthrough simulations revealed that UTSA-120a possessed a $\tau_{\mathrm{break}}$ value two times longer than SIFSIX-2-Cu-i for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ along with a slightly higher $\tau_{\mathrm{break}}$ value for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . Similarly, experimental breakthrough studies exhibited 86 and 36  min $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ retention times in the sample bed for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ (v/v 15:85) and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ $(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}~50{:}50)$ mixtures at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1  bar, respectively, whereas $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ rapidly came out of the bed with a high purity, as depicted in Figure  44e-f, proving the excellent separation performance of UTSA-120. In terms of overall potentiality, in addition to high sorption capacity and separation selectivity, easy regenerability and excellent recyclability are also necessary and demanding. Interestingly, UTSA-120 displayed easy regenerability (by $15~\mathrm{min}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ with a He flow rate of $20\ \mathrm{mL}\ \mathrm{min^{-1}})$ with almost no significant loss of recyclability up to three cycles (Figure $44\mathrm{g})$ , which made this material promising for use in flue and biogas separation. Additionally, UTSA-120 exhibited ultrahigh water (1 month) and moisture stabilities ( $80\%$ RH for 1 day and more than 6 months in air), which is necessary, as water and/or moisture are omnipresent during such separation processes. This study signified the efficiency of the in situ study with a partnership of experimental study to elucidate the sorption mechanism at the molecular level.
Along the same line, by employing the “Selectivity triggers through combined approach (OMSs and surface functionalization)” approach Ou et al. constructed a zeolite-like 3D gyroidal $\mathrm{cu}$ -MOF, SCNU-1, which possessed spherical polyhedral cagebased $(1.8\times1.6\times1.4\mathrm{nm}^{3}.$ ) 1D nanochannels (diameter $0.5\mathrm{nm}$ ) decorated with 2-coordinated $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ OMSs and uncoordinated hydroxyl groups for surface functionalization.[446] SCNU-1 displayed an ultrahigh $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding affinity and exhibited strong host-guest interactions due to its dual functionality, as evidenced by in situ IR spectroscopy. Interestingly, the framework selectively captured targeted $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ guest molecules at 1.78  mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ under 1  bar pressure at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ , whereas the competing gas $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ uptake was negligible (almost zero) and further led to a flue gas $[\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\ (\mathsf{v/v}\ 15{:}85)]$ separation selectivity of 108 at 298 K, as calculated through IAST selectivity (Table 4, Sl. No 44). In addition to selective sorption capacity and separation selectivity, SCNU-1 exhibited excellent thermal stability (until 773 K) and chemical stability by retaining structural integrity even after exposure to several common organic solvents, a wide $\mathrm{\pH}$ range (2-14) and $24\mathrm{~h~}$ stability in boiling water, as verified by PXRD analysis. Additionally, the $304~\mathrm{K}$ gas cycling $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption−desorption process exhibited no significant loss of potentiality (only $2.8~\mathrm{wt\%}$ weight loss) (Figure 45a), which further confirmed the easy regenerability and excellent recyclability along with the framework robustness in this direction. Furthermore, the in situ IR spectroscopy study displayed the appearance of new sorption peaks at 3597, 3625, 3700, and $3725~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which could be ascribable to the $_\mathrm{H-OCO}_{2}$ group’s stretching vibration (Figure  45b). Additionally, the redshifted bands (at 2329, $2343\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ and blueshifted bands (at 2353, $2368~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ with respect to the unperturbed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ asymmetric stretch value $(2349~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ further confirmed the strong host-guest interactions between $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules and hydroxyl groups and $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ OMSs, respectively. Thus, this study helped to understand the structure-property relationship through an in situ IR spectroscopy study with the partnership of experimental results and made SCNU-1 viable for the flue gas separation direction.
In 2019, Bai et al. constructed two stable 3D cationic porous Sc-frameworks, NJU-Bai50 and NJU-Bai51, employing the “Selectivity triggers through combined approach (OMSs and charged skeleton)” approach, which possessed a high density of OMSs, and the sorption mechanism was elucidated at the molecular level via in situ SCXRD and in situ FT-IR structural analysis.[258] SCXRD structural analysis revealed that each metal center was coordinated with two water molecules, whereas ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ anions were present preferentially inside the cages and made the overall framework neutral. This slight increase in metal-metal separation in NJU-Bai51 (from $6.{\overset{\circ}{4}}5{\mathrm{~\AA~}}$ to 6.75 Å) compared to NJU-Bai50 enabled 72 times higher $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake $(0.13\mathrm{~mmol~g^{-1}})$ for NJU-Bai51 even at low pressure (0.4 mbar) than NJU-Bai50 (0.0018  mmol $\ensuremath{\mathbf{g}}^{-1})$ at 298 K. Moreover, the
IAST selectivity calculation at 298 K displayed a 51 times higher flue gas $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (v/v 15:85)] separation selectivity of 545.7 for NJU-Bai51 than NJU-Bai50, whereas the biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ $(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}$ 50:50)] separation selectivity was 10.7 and 4.4 for NJU-Bai51 and NJU-Bai50, respectively (Table  3, Sl. No 50–51). Additionally, both frameworks displayed excellent water (1 month exposure) and $\mathrm{\pH}$ stability (1-12) and made these materials promising for practical flue gas separation, as such separations often need to be performed in moist conditions. Furthermore, to assess practical separation performance, the dynamic breakthrough separation study for NJU-Bai51 was performed at 298 K not only in dry condition flue and biogas binary mixtures but also in humid $5\%$ water vapor) condition flue gas mixtures, as displayed in Figure 46a. The breakthrough curve displayed complete separation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules compared with other competing $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ gas molecules with a breakthrough time of 20 and $18\ \mathrm{min}$ for dry and humid flue gas, respectively, whereas it was $8~\mathrm{min}$ for dry biogas. However, at $153\ \mathrm{K},$ , in situ SCXRD structural analysis of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -loaded framework displayed that the guest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule interacted with two OMSs ${\mathrm{Sc}}^{3+}$ metal centers at a time having molecular formula of NJU-Bai51a· $(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{1.0}$ (Figure 46b), which was also a good agreement with the experimental one $\phantom{-}1.08\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ molecule captured at 0.15  bar and 298 K). Interestingly, the Sc–O (O from captured $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules) distance was found to be $2.0{\bar{5}}{\mathrm{~\AA}},$ shorter than the van der Waals radii sum of Sc and O (3.5 Å) and attested to the strong host-guest binding strength between the OMSs of the framework and captured $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules. In situ FTIR study of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -loaded NJU-Bai51 exhibited a newer peak at $2332~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ corroborated to the asymmetric ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{O}}}={\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}}}={\boldsymbol{\mathrm{O}}}$ stretching frequency of entrapped $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules, as displayed in Figure 46c.
In 2011, employing the “Selectivity triggers through combined approach (surface functionalization and charged skeleton)” approach, Zhang et al. developed a 2D anionic Co-MOF, MAF-X7, which possessed partially exposed uncoordinated N heteroatoms and preferentially captured $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ .[397] The sorption mechanism was elucidated through in situ SCXRD structural analysis of the gas-loaded samples. At $298\mathrm{~K~},$ , $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption exhibited almost four times higher uptake $(26.1\mathrm{cc}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ than the competing $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules $(7\mathrm{cc}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ under 1 bar pressure and displayed selective capture of targeted $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules. IAST selectivity calculation at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ revealed a biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ (v/v 50:50)] separation selectivity of 22, as tabulated in Table 4 (Sl. No 26). However, to elucidate the sorption mechanism, in situ SCXRD analysis of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -loaded sample was performed, in which a difference electron density map $\left(F_{O^{-}}F_{c}\right)$ showed that three electron peaks were present and arranged linearly inside the pores of MAF-X7 (Figure 47a), implying the successful loading of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules. An in situ SCXRD study also revealed that $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules preferentially interacted with triazolate 4-nitrogen via a $\mathbf{C}\cdot\cdot\cdot\mathbf{N}$ interaction with a distance of 3.26(4) Å (Figure $476$ ). Even though the C of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule interacted with the phenyl carbon, the much longer C···C interacting distance (3.65(4) Å) than the sum of the van der Waals radii $(3.40\mathrm{~\AA})$ confirmed that $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules were likely bonded with uncoordinated triazolate nitrogen atoms. Additionally, the O of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule interacted with the Hdmf+ cation via $\textsf{C}\cdot\cdot\cdot\cup$ interactions with a distance of 3.84(3) Å. Therefore, from the in situ SCXRD study, the high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding affinity of MAF-X7 could be attributed to the combined effect of the exposed uncoordinated N heteroatom and the charged skeleton, which further efficiently triggered biogas separation selectivity.
## 6. Real-Time Potential Assessment with Breakthrough Experiments Under Dry and Wet Conditions
As mentioned earlier, moisture is a troublesome component in flue gas stream (often contains $8–10\%\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ and biogas $5.10\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ mixture, which reduces the separation performance of developed MOFs significantly. As MOFs are infamous for their instability in water, effective design and development of moisture and/or water-stable MOFs is on-demand for their practical implementations where a moist or an aqueous environment is concerned. $[459,470]$ During $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ separation process, the omnipresent $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules compete with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules for the same adsorption sites, and thus causing a lower performance. Additionally, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules disrupt the metal-organic ligands (e.g., carboxylate) coordination bonds and thus disintegrating the MOF structure. To date, except some $\mathrm{Zr^{IV}}$ and $\mathrm{Hf^{\mathrm{1\bar{V}}}}.$ -based MOFs, imidazole-based ZIFs, MILs, and PCNs, there are quite a few reports on water-stable MOFs, and most of them are recently developed.[93] Thus, to enhance the water-stability of the developed framework quite a few strategies have been adopted such as (i) high valence metal ions coordinated with the carboxylate ligands (hard-hard combination); (ii) using low valent metal ions to coordinate with N donors (soft-soft combination); (iii) immobilization of hydrophobic functional groups on the backbone.[93] In one hand, OMSs are advantageous to realize selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture than competing gas molecules under dry conditions; on the other hand they impose a limitation while moisture is introduced into the gas streams for the said separations because under wet condition OMSs easily coordinate the water molecules and thus not only leading to reduced separation performance but also making the MOFs unstable. Thus extreme care must be taken in designing MOFs while taking advantage of OMSs for preferential capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from flue and biogas separations in presence of real separation condition (wet condition). It is worthy to mention that none of the MOFs presented under Section  4.2 with the section heading “Selectivity triggers through open metal sites (OMS)” considered the humidity effect on flue and biogas separation performances.
## 7. MOF-Based Mixed Matrix Membranes (MMMs) for Flue and Biogas Separation
Both adsorption-based and membrane-based gas separations are advantageous over other traditional techniques in terms of cost effectivity because of significant reductions in the energy intake, though they do have the limitation of low selectivity of the adsorbents and/or membranes materials. However, ease of operation, low capital investment, low maintenance need, greater process flexibility, and low ecological footprint made membrane-based gas separation even more attractive. Alongside, membranes are compact and lightweight, can be installed easily either horizontally or vertically.
Although flue and biogas separations by membrane-based polymers are very well-known from long back, the major drawback for most of the cases is the failure of maintaining a trade-off between permeability and separation selectivity.[472] Additionally, brittleness and defects often limit the commercialization of membranes. More than $90\%$ of commercial membranes are still constructed from less than ten membrane materials, which are being used for decades.[472,473] Hence, to improve the overall performance of these polymeric membranes, effective design and fabrication of such membranes by introducing variety of filler materials are considered as a promising approach. Till now most commonly used fillers are metal oxides nanoparticles, clays, Pd, zero-valent iron (ZVI), single and multi-walled carbon nanotubes (SWCNTs and MWCNTs), carbon molecular sieves, magnetic iron-based nanoparticles, silicate materials, non-porous silica, porous silica, and MOFs.[473] As the performance of the fabricated mixed matrix membranes (MMMs) depends upon the physicochemical properties of the fillers along with filler-matrix interaction, polymer matrix’s nature, and processing methods, judicious selection of filler material is very crucial and thus demanding too. Employing polymeric membranes as support and MOFs as a filler to construct MOFbased MMMs for flue and biogas gas separation has become an intense area of research in MOFs and material science communities.
Although MOFs possess a wide range of chemistry, structural variability, and pore sizes, which made them suitable candidates for membrane construction, a limited number of MOF membranes were fabricated till date to check their potentiality toward flue and biogas separation direction. Importantly, the ability to modify the pore size and shape of MOF materials provides a significant advantage in achieving the necessary gas separation based on gas molecule size. However, the disadvantages are the grain boundary effects and formation of pinholes/cracks during membrane preparation, which reduce the expected selectivity significantly and are unable to attain Robeson’s upper bound selectivity/permeability lines. Fortunately, judicious selection of appropriate MOF/polymer pairs for the targeted gas molecules separation should not be a great challenge due to the presence of hundreds of available polymers and an unlimited number of MOFs, though solution processability of MOFs is a disadvantage. Although computational studies can predict the selectivity and permeability of $\mathsf{M O F}/$ polymer MMMs to some extent, the enormous experimental efforts are highly demandable. As far as flue and biogas separations are concerned, 56 examples of MOF-based MMMs (till June 2021) are reported in literature. Flue and biogas separation with MMMs is one type of membrane separation with MOFs. The question arises why not to choose pure MOF membranes constructed by MOF intergrowths for flue and biogas separations? Unfortunately, till now, no such membranes developed solely from MOFs are reported in literature for flue and biogas separations.
Generally, with the increase in permeability, separation selectivity decreases, and the membrane thickness is inversely proportional to permeability. Therefore, the constructed MMMs are necessarily to be thin with large membrane areas to maximize both the permeability and separation selectivity. By thorough investigation, we found a very limited number of MOFbased MMMs (Table 7), which were employed for flue gas and/ or biogas separation. Although the number is relatively higher for biogas separation than the flue gas, the overall number is significantly less than the developed MOFs studied for adsorption-based separations. This dictates that the effective design and development of MOF-based MMMs are still in early-stage, and demands enormous attention. As mentioned earlier, in this review our main focus is especially on developed MOFs employed for flue and biogas separation over the years through adsorption-based technique; however, considering the importance and urgency to develop MOF-based MMMs, we have discussed a few examples in this section to highlight the potentiality and necessity of MOF-based MMMs fabrication for flue and biogas separation as well. Also, we have provided a critical discussion on MOF-based MMMs with the help of standard
Robeson’s plot in the critical analysis section and pointed out the future goal in the future prospects section.
Membranes are fascinating for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ separation, primarily because of their inherent permeating properties. As listed in Table  2, kinetic diameter of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is smaller than $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and thus, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ diffuses faster in membrane materials. Additionally, higher molecular weight and larger quadruple moment of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (please see Table  2) expedites it to naturally adsorb more strongly or to dissolve at greater concentrations in membranes compared to $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . Such features cause high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ permeation rates and selectivities over $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ .
In 2018, Hou et  al. reported MOF-based MMMs for flue $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (v/v 15:85)] and biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ $\left(\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}~50{:}50)\right]$ separation, where MOFs UiO-66, UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , and UiO-66–COOH were used as fillers and Pebax as a polymer support.[474] Although the BET surface area decreased for the functionalized UiO-66 (1800, 1213, $400~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for UiO-66, UiO- $66\substack{\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ , UiO${\mathsf{J}}6{\mathsf{-}}({\mathsf{C O O H}})_{2})$ respectively), the $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ functionalized UiO-66 exhibited improved $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity due to the presence of polar surface functionality, as discussed previously in this review. Although a large percentage of MOF nanofillers incorporation into the matrix is very challenging as they often lead to the loss of mechanical strength and non-homogeneous distribution, taking the advantage of ultra-high stability of UiO-66, $80\mathrm{\wt\%}$ of UiO-66 were incorporated into the thin Pebax layer. The effect of surface functionalization on $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ separation performances were analyzed in both pure and mixed gases. The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ permeability of UiO-66/Pebax-1657-based composite membranes were much higher than the other competing gas molecules $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}^{\mathbf{\Lambda}}$ , which could be attributed to the stronger affinity of rubbery PEO block in Pebax toward the polar $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas molecules over non-polar competing gas molecules. Although, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ permeability was slightly increased for UiO-66/Pebax-1657-based composite membranes with the increasing of MOF loading percentage, the permeability for UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}/$ Pebax-1657-based composite membranes were significantly increased with the increment of loading $\%$ , as displayed in Figure 48a. The permeability increment with the increase of MOF loading percentage could be attributed to the combined effect of effectively increased crystallinity and the rigidified polymeric chains. However, the highest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ separation selectivity were obtained for 50 wt% MOF loading, as depicted in Figure 48b, c and could be attributed to the good compatibility between the nanofillers and polymeric matrix than other $w t\%$ variants. At $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under 2 bar pressure, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ separation selectivity was 57 for UiO- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}/$ Pebax-1657-based composite membrane, higher than UiO-66/ Pebax-1657-based composite membrane and UiO-66- $(\mathrm{COOH})_{2}/$ Pebax-1657-based composite membranes (45 and 50, respectively) (Table 7, Sl. No 1–3). The improved separation selectivity for U $\mathrm{.O.66\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}/$ Pebax-1657-based composite membranes than other composite membranes ascribed to the polar functionality presence and higher $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ solubility within the thin selective layer. Furthermore, the polymer matrix composite membranes were exposed to different feed pressures (up to 15 bar) in the complete pressurization–depressurization cycle for the investigation of the swelling/compaction effect. The pure Pebax-coated membrane and MOF incorporated composite membranes displayed decreasing trend for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ permeability with the increment of feed pressure (Figure  48d), which is due to the competing effects of plasticization and hydrostatic pressure. However, at elevated pressure, MOF incorporated composite membranes exhibited a slight decrease in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ permeability over pure Pebax-coated membrane, which is due to the reduction of compaction effect by enhancing the rigidity of the polymer matrix and further signified the necessity of the MOF-based MMMs fabrication.
In a remarkable work, to obtain the enhanced biogas $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/$ $\mathrm{CH}_{4}\ \mathrm{(v/v}\ 50{:}50)]$ separation selectivity, in 2019, Khan et al. fabricated Bio-MOF-1 loaded polysulfone (PSf) MMMs in which BioMOF was used as a filler (with various $w t\%$ ) material in MMMs construction.[475] To analyze the separation performance, three different $w t\%$ (10, 20, and 30) PSf/Bio-MOF-1 MMMs were constructed among which PSf/Bio-MOF-1 $(30\mathrm{wt\%})$ exhibited $168\%$ enhanced $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ permeability along with $58\%$ enhanced separation selectivity with respect to the pristine polymer membrane PSf. The separation performances were investigated not only in the binary equimolar gas mixture but also at a different feed concentration of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , and in various operating temperatures. An increased permeability was observed with the increase of filler concentration, which signified the intrinsic permeability of the filler. Although a substantial increment of permeability was observed even at the minimum loading Bio-MOF $w t\%$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 10 bar pressure, the maximum permeability for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was observed for PSf/Bio-MOF-1 $(30~\mathrm{wt\%})$ MMM (16.57 Barrer) (Figure $49\mathrm{a}$ , Table 7, Sl. No 5). Such a huge permeability increment (from 6.95 for pure PSf to 16.57 Barrer for $30\mathrm{wt\%}$ MMM) could be attributed to the generation of the additional channels with a free volume increase in PSf matrix, which further facilitated the efficient diffusion of the targeted gas molecules in the MMMs. The higher permeability of polar $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules than the competing $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules could be attributed to the preferential adsorption onto the polar pore surface of the Bio-MOF-1. Interestingly, the increment of permeability led to the enhancement of biogas separation selectivity. The highest separation selectivity (39) of PSf/Bio-MOF-1 $(30~\mathrm{wt\%})$ MMM (Table  7, Sl. No 5) over other variants signified the good compatibility of MOF filler and PSf polymer matrix (Figure 49a). Also, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ solubility coefficients and diffusion coefficients were increased with the loading $\%$ , which indicated that prepared MMMs were defect-free and captured targeted gas molecules transported smoothly. Around $69\%$ increment of diffusion coefficient $(6.05\times10^{-8}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ and $3.59\times10^{-8}\ \mathrm{cm^{2}\ s^{-1}}$ for $30\mathrm{\wt\%}$ MMM and pure PSf membrane, respectively) along with $42\%$ increment of solubility coefficient in PSf/Bio-MOF-1 $(30\mathrm{\wt\%})$ MMM $(2.74\times10^{2}\mathrm{cm}^{3}(\mathrm{STP})/\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{cmHg})$ than the pure PSf membrane $(1.94\times10^{2}\mathrm{~cm}^{3}(\mathrm{STP})/\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{cmHg})$ are indicative of the smooth transportation of the captured gases through the easy passage offered by the porous MOF fillers. Furthermore, to investigate the temperature effect on the membrane permeation performance, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ permeability was tested from the temperature $298\mathrm{~K~}$ to $338\mathrm{~K~}$ in which $191\%$ increment of permeability was observed for PSf/Bio-MOF-1 $(30~\mathrm{wt\%})$ MMM than the pure PSf membrane (Figure  49b). The increased permeability with the increase of temperature could be attributed to the increased flexibility of the polymer chain, which offered faster diffusion of the gas molecules with improved mobility. Also, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ permeation’s activation energy calculation displayed significantly lower activation energies for the MMMs (2.12, 4, and 5.25 kJ mol–1 for 30, 20 and $10\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ MMMs, respectively) than the pure PSf membrane $(7~\mathrm{kJ~mol^{-1}})$ , which signified the effectivity and urgency of MMMs construction for enhanced biogas separation.
$\mathrm{In}2015$ , Li et al. constructed three polysulfone-based MMMs, $\mathrm{PSF–NH_{2}–M I L–1}25(\mathrm{Ti})–10\%$ , ${\mathrm{PSF}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)- $20\%$ and, $\mathrm{PSF–NH_{2}–M I L–1}25(\mathrm{Ti})–30\%,$ , using various $w t\%$ (10, 20 and 30) of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) as a MOF filler and studied high-pressure equimolar biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}(v/v~50{:}50)]}$ separation performance of the MMMs.[480] At $303\mathrm{~K~}$ temperature and 3 bar pressure the permeation study displayed the enhanced permeation of the gas molecules, which was directly proportional with the MOF loading percentage (Table 7, Sl. No 13–15). Around $320\%$ of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ permeation enhancement was observed for the ${\mathrm{PSF}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ -MIL125(Ti)- $30\%$ MMM than the pristine membrane, which could be attributed to the adsorption-controlled permeation mechanism. Although the maximum $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ permeability enhancement was observed for ${\mathrm{PSF}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)- $30\%$ MMM than the other MMMs variants, the other competing $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ gas permeation was also enhanced significantly $(219\%)$ than the pristine membrane, which further reduced the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ separation factor and unable to maintain the trade-off between the permeability and separation selectivity. The maximum $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ separation factor was observed for ${\mathrm{PSF}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)- $20\%$ MMM with a value of 29.5 at $303\mathrm{~K~}$ and 3 bar (Table 7, Sl. No 14). This result signified the good compatibility between the fillers and the PSF polymer at $20\mathrm{\wt\%}$ , whereas the selectivity decrease at $30\mathrm{\mt}\%$ loading than $20\mathrm{\mt}\%$ loadings could be attributed to the declination of diffusion selectivity. For natural gas upgradation in industrial applications, high-pressure membrane separation is really necessary. Interestingly, $\mathrm{PSF}{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ MIL-125(Ti)- $20\%$ MMM possessed a negligible loss in permeability and separation factor even at 10 bar pressure, which further evident $20~\mathrm{wt\%}$ MMM as the best performing candidate over the other fabricated MMM variants. Although at 30  bar pressure the permeability of ${\mathrm{PSF}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)- $20\%$ MMM decreased to some extent, the value was still higher than the pure PSF membrane. However, good separation performance was also maintained at 30  bar pressure, which further made this MMM promising toward practical biogas separation.
To evaluate the biogas $\mathrm{[CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ (v/v 50:50)] separation of the MOF-based MMMs, in 2018, Neves et  al. constructed Matrimid5218 based MMMs, Matrimid5218/Fe(BTC), where Fe(BTC) at different loading percentages (10, 20, and $30\%$ ) was used as filler materials through solvent evaporation method.[481] Incorporation of Fe(BTC) in Matrimid5218 membrane not only enhanced the gas permeability and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ separation selectivity but also enhanced the hydrophobicity and rigidity of the MMMs. The temperature impact on gas permeability and separation selectivity was verified by analyzing the performance at 303 K, $323\mathrm{~K~}$ , and $353\mathrm{~K~}$ temperatures. Although permeability was increased for both the gases $(\Nu_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}^{\cdot}$ ) with the increment of loading percentage at $303\mathrm{~K~}$ and 0.7 bar pressure, the permeability increment of $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ was significantly lower than the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules (Figure 50a). The high permeability of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas molecules in MMMs could be attributed to the electrostatic interaction of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules with the OMSs of the Fe(BTC) and the increment of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ solubility due to the increase of d-spacing in the Matrimid5218 membrane. The biogas $[\mathsf{C O}_{2}/$ $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ (v/v 50:50)] separation selectivity was also enhanced with the increase of temperature and the maximum separation selectivity was obtained for $10\mathrm{\mt\%}$ loading at $353\mathrm{~K~}$ temperature (Table  7, Sl. No 16–18), which could be attributed to the good compatibility between the fillers and the Matrimid5218 polymer. Although the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ separation selectivity of $30\mathrm{\wt\%}$ loadings MMM was slightly lower at $353\mathrm{~K~}$ than the $10\mathrm{\mt}\%$ loadings (Table 7, Sl. No 16–18), both the MMMs surpassed the Robeson upper-bound correlation, as depicted in Figure  50b. Hence, this study demonstrated the effectiveness of the MOFbased MMMs fabrication for post-combustion $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture.
In 2019, Smith et  al. fabricated HKUST-1-based 6FDADAM polymeric MMMs with various filler loading percentages (10, 20, and 30) and evaluated their $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/$ $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ separation performances.[482] The fillers were synthesized by employing a new synthetic strategy considering the modulator-based approach and according to the traditional synthesis of HKUST-1 particles, which are termed as branched HKUST-1 and bulk HKUST-1, respectively. The gas separation performance was analyzed for both the branched HKUST-1 and bulk HKUST-1 filler-based MMMs. Interestingly, with the increase of filler loading percentage, gas permeability was also increased in which $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ permeability was increased almost 2.5 fold (2500 Barrer) at 15 psi than the pure polymer 6FDA-DAM for $30\%$ branched HKUST-1 loaded MMM. Although $30\%$ bulk HKUST-1 loaded MMM possessed almost similar enhancement of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ permeability, the separation selectivity at $35^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 15 psi was decreased significantly $[20\pm1$ to $14.9\pm0.7$ for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4})$ than the $30\%$ branched HKUST-1 loaded MMM ( $[20~\pm~1~$ to $16.5~\pm~0.8$ for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $17.7\ \pm\ 0.9$ to $16.3~\pm~0.8$ for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ separations) (Table  7, Sl. No 20–25). Relatively high performance of the branched HKUST-1 loaded MMMs than the bulk HKUST-1 loaded MMMs could be attributed to the higher compatibility between the polymeric membrane and MOF fillers along with the lower degree of interfacial defects. Moreover, branched HKUST-1 loaded MMMs exhibited a consistently increasing trend in effective diffusivities and solubilities for the gas molecules, whereas bulk HKUST-1 loaded MMMs did not display any clear trend, which further evident the lower degree of interfacial defects for branched HKUST-1 loaded MMMs. As the separation selectivity loss was relatively lower for the branched HKUST-1 loaded MMMs than the bulk HKUST-1 loaded MMMs with the increasing of filler loading percentages, the branched MMMs resided closer to the Robeson upper bound limit than the bulk MMMs. Overall, a good tradeoff between gas permeability and separation selectivity of the branched HKUST-1 loaded MMMs could be observed.
As the compatibility between the polymeric membrane and MOF fillers plays a pivotal role to obtain the maximum performance of MOF-based MMMs, in 2013, Long et  al. fabricated three $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ MOF-based MMMs by using three different polymers [polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS), cross-linked polyethylene oxide (XLPEO), and polyimide ( $\mathrm{PI}=6\mathrm{I}$ FDA-TMPDA)] and verified their performances for flue gas $[\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (v/v 15:85)] separation.[484] Although an improved separation selectivity was observed for MMMs constructed from rubbery polymers (PDMS and XLPEO), the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ permeability was decreased than the pure polymers, which could be attributed to the pore plugging by the flexible rubbery polymer chains due to having smaller width than the $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ MOF’s channel diameter. However, MMM constructed by glassy PI polymer not only exhibited enhanced permeability (650 to 850 barrers for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{)}$ but also displayed enhanced separation selectivity (from 14 to 23 at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at 2 bar) (Table 7, Sl. No 32–34). $10\mathrm{\wt\%}$ $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})/$ PI membrane were able to maintain the trade-off between the gas permeability and separation selectivity than the other fabricated MMMs, which signified the better compatibility of the polymeric membrane and MOF fillers due to the presence of rigid aromatic and imide rings in the glassy PI polymer backbone. The higher performance of $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})/\mathrm{PI}$ MMM than the other MMMs could be attributed not only to the enhancement of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ solubility in PI membrane upon the incorporation of MOF fillers but also almost unchanged $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ diffusivity. However, a significant decrease in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ diffusivity was observed for MMMs constructed from the rubbery polymers, which indicated the outer pore windows plugging of the $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}\mathbf{\upmu}$ (dobdc) by the polymer chains. Among these three different polymer-based MMMs $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})/\mathrm{PI}$ MMM displayed relatively closer to the Robeson upper bound limit than the other MMM variants (Figure 51), which further signified the effectivity and necessity of the judicious selection of appropriate polymeric membrane.
In 2019, Zou and Zhu et  al. constructed MMMs, UiO$66{\cdot}\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{sPIM-1}$ , with different $w t\%$ of UiO-66-CN MOF loadings in which MOF has been used as a filler and sPIM-1 as a support.[498] By varying the MOF contents in the MMMs, gas permeability and flue gas separation selectivity were analyzed in which the maximum potentiality was obtained for 20 $w t\%$ MOF loading. The constructed MMM displayed excellent flue gas $\left(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2};\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v}\ 15/85\right)$ ) separation selectivity of 33.7 with a high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ permeability of 10 389 Barrer at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1.4  bar (Table  7, Sl. No 60), attributed to the superior compatibility of the two components of the MMM. Moreover, flue gas separation performance was evaluated in presence of water $\mathrm{(CO_{2}/N_{2}/H_{2}O}$ v/v/v 14/81/5) and $\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ gas mixtures $\mathrm{(CO_{2}/N_{2}/S O_{2}}$ $\mathrm{v/v/v~15/85/800~ppm)}$ . The outstanding performance retention even after exposure of water and $\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ (separation selectivity of 32.8 and 33.2 with a high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ permeability of 9095.4 and
10 354 Barrer, respectively, at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1.4  bar) proved its potentiality toward real world flue gas separation which could be attributed to the hydrophobicity of sPIM-1 and high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ affinity in the MMM. This is the only study which considered the flue gas performance evaluation of MMMs in presence of water and acidic gas as well.
## 8. Critical Analysis
Even though we have made the entire discussions critically under eight different approaches and their subsequent subsections both for adsorption-based and membrane-based flue and biogas separations with MOFs by analyzing every materials thoroughly, there is still some room to provide more crucial insights on these materials and to make connections among them, which will be pointed out in this section. From the above discussions, it is clear that to obtain the trade-off between selective capture of targeted gas molecules and separation selectivity, fine-tuning of the pore size is indeed necessary for pore confinement effect, as it can restrict to capture the competing gas molecules through size-exclusion principle. The adsorption mechanism (physical or chemical) could be clearly understood by careful observation of the interactions between adsorbed gas molecules and the pore surface of the adsorbent materials. The physisorbents (physical adsorbents) capture $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules by physical interaction depending upon the charge attraction, especially, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -surface van der Waals interactions, as well as pole-ion or pole-pole interactions between the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ quadrupole and the surface ionic or polar sites. However, these interactions are very weak and lead to the low heats of adsorption,[499] which is very beneficial for easy regenerability while applying minimal energy and therefore could be readily implemented using PSA. Nevertheless, design and development of MOFs with controllable pore size is very challenging but demanding too. Even though larger pore can accommodate more number of guest gas molecules, the problem is that they are inefficient to capture targeted gas molecules selectively, as larger pores are very reluctant to enable pore confinement. However, it is quite interesting that despite having relatively larger pore size, if any framework possesses a specific binding site and/or polar pore surface, it will be equally capable to display high separation selectivity, as elaborately discussed in the earlier sections. Undoubtedly, presence of specific binding sites such as OMSs and/or surface functionalization can selectively capture targeted gas molecules but they are often associated to chemisorption, which leads the regeneration and recycle process energy and cost-intensive. As physisorption is an inherently exothermic process, heating management in adsorption process is very crucial to obtain the maximum working capacity of sorbent. Although chemisorbents can exhibit high isosteric heats of adsorption along with superior selectivity, the major disadvantage is the energyintensive regeneration process such as thermal swing adsorption (TSA), which is a time-consuming process technology too. Now, the question is “What will make MOF as a perfect sorbent for flue and biogas separations?” A perfect sorbent need to possess high working capacity (e.g., high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity), high adsorption and desorption kinetics, excellent separation selectivity, moisture and/or water stability (preferably toward mild acidic condition considering presence of acid gas impurities), easy regenerability and magnificent recyclability along with trade-off among all these parameters at a time. If a MOF material can satisfy all these parameters exclusively, it will have a high potential to be employed for practical application. For the sake of interest to verify the practical efficiency of the developed MOFs for successful industrial employment, we not only compared their sorption capacity versus separation selectivity values (Figure 52) along with separation selectivity versus binding affinity ( $\cdot Q_{\mathrm{st}}$ values) (Figure 53) but also plotted separation selectivity versus experimental breakthrough times (Figure 54), which will surely provide a deeper insight into the current status of the developed MOF materials. According to the literature reports, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ separation selectivity needs to be above 500 along with a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ loading (or sorption capacity) of 2–4  mmol $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ from the mixed gas phase for practical applicability and in terms of economic perspectives.[413,500] Thus, we have plotted mixed gas phase $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity versus separation selectivity, as displayed in Figure  52a. As most of the cases, the mixed gas phase $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity was not reported, for the performance analysis we have selected only those MOFs where the values are either mentioned or can be identified from the selectivity plots. It should be noted that for better visualization in figures we have considered the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ separation selectivity values above 30 and the temperature has been considered at ambient temperature (293-298 K). Although Qc-5-Cu-sql- $\beta$ displayed significantly higher separation selectivity value (40 000) than the minimum desired value and the other developed MOFs (Figure  52a), its mixed gas phase $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity is not up to the desired level $(0.8~\mathrm{mmol~g^{-1}})$ . Similarly, the mixed gas phase $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity of 1.S $(3.5\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ lies in the desirable region but the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ separation selectivity value is relatively lower (200) than the targeted value (Figure 52a). Thus, both these two materials are incapable to maintain the trade-off between sorption capacity and separation selectivity in terms of economic perspectives, which is the paramount target. However, $\mathrm{\Gamma}[\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{bcppm})\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}]{\cdot}a$ c exhibited the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity of 1.04 mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ separation selectivity of 590 (Figure 52a), which can be considered relatively better trade-off material over others, whereas other materials $\mathrm{{Mn}_{13}(L)}$ , Cd(OBA), $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -MTABA, IITKGP-5, IITKGP-9, IITKGP-11, IITKGP-12, UPC-70, UTSA-49 suffer from high sorption capacity and separation selectivity as well. Therefore, there is a huge scope and urgency to develop well-performing MOFs for this flue gas separation. For clear visualization of what should be our ultimate target in MOFs’ development for flue gas separations, we have provided an ideal plot mentioning the desired trade-off region in green color (Figure  52c). Developed MOFs which will fall either in pink or blue shaded regions, will not be efficient for this projected application, as they will suffer low sorption capacity and low separation selectivity, respectively. Thus, our target should be developing MOFs, which will lie in the green shaded region by maintaining excellent trade-off between these two.
Unfortunately, such a target of separation selectivity and mixed phase sorption capacity for biogas separation is yet to be set. However, to analyze the current status of the developed MOFs employed for biogas separation, we plotted Figure  52b considering the selectivity value higher than 20 at ambient temperature, i.e., above 293 K. As displayed in the figure, MUF-16 and Qc-5-Cu-sql- $\cdot\beta$ were efficient to exhibit not only high mixed gas phase $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity (1.8 and $1.6~\mathrm{mmol~g^{-1}}$ , respectively) but also remarkable $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ separation selectivity (6690 and 3300, respectively) over the others, whereas 1·S, $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{L})$ , MAF-X7, and $\mathrm{{{Zn}_{14}(L)_{6}}}$ suffered from both sorption capacity and separation selectivity. Although $\mathrm{{Zn}}_{4}\mathrm{{O}}(\mathrm{{L}})_{2}$ and 5a displayed higher mixed gas phase $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity, their separation selectivity value is significantly lower than the maximum obtained selectivity value of MUF-16. As no such target in these parameters is yet available, thus, it is inappropriate to make comment on the potential of developed MOFs for biogas separation at industrial scale. Thus collaborative academia and industry efforts need to be sought, considering the massive scope of further development to this area.
In order to have favorable thermodynamically energy-efficient regeneration of MOFs, $\boldsymbol{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ values is necessary to be within the range of $35{-}50\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ for ultramicroporous $(<0.7\ \mathrm{nm})$ materials. Hence, the material possessing high separation selectivity but displaying relatively lower $\boldsymbol{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ values will be efficient for cost-effective recyclable purpose, which is also very necessary in terms of overall potentiality. However, lower $\varrho_{\mathrm{st}}$ value often leads to lower sorption capacity and lower separation selectivity as well due to low binding affinity, which make them inefficient toward this projected direction. Therefore, developing MOFs having well-balance in high separation selectivity and lower $\boldsymbol{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ value is indispensable. To verify this, we have plotted separation selectivity versus $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding affinity ( $\cdot Q_{\mathrm{st}}$ values) of the top performing MOFs, as displayed in Figure 53.
From the Figure 53a, b, it is clear that the regeneration cost will be lower for Qc-5-Cu-sql- $\beta$ and MUF-16 with advantage of having highest selectivity value among all. Even though NENU520, 5a, $\mathrm{Zn(HL)(bipy)}$ and $\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{HL})_{2}$ exhibited significantly lower $\boldsymbol{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ values and the regeneration cost will be minimum for these cases, they suffer from low selectivity value, which is one of the prime concerns for industrial utilization of such materials. Employing both of Qc-5-Cu-sql- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\beta$ and MUF-16 toward flue and biogas separation can be effective choice in terms of regeneration cost-effectivity, however, both of them do not seem to be promising in terms of amount adsorbed in mixed gas phase (Figure  52a, b). Besides, sorption capacity, separation selectivity and cost-effective regenerability, longer breakthrough time is also equally important, as longer breakthrough time can only reduce the necessity of frequent regeneration of the MOF bed. Considering this, we have plotted separation selectivity versus experimental breakthrough times, as depicted in Figure  54. However, it should be noted that breakthrough time is dependent on several parameters such as bed length/diameter, flow rate, gas pressure, packing density etc. It was observed that Qc-5-Cu-sql- $\cdot\beta$ displayed excellent trade-off between flue gas separation selectivity and breakthrough times, whereas a moderate trade-off was also observed for biogas separation. Even though IITKGP-12 and Cu-1 possessed higher breakthrough times than other developed MOFs, they were incapable to display remarkable separation selectivity and unlikely to employ toward this direction practically. Hence, this critical analysis revealed that maintaining trade-off among high sorption capacity, separation selectivity, easy regenerability and longer breakthrough time all together will lead overall maximum potentiality. Therefore, MOF development for this challenging flue and biogas separation is ever urgent yet largely unmet and there is huge scope and urgency for further development while considering their plausible usage in separation industry.
On the other hand, the MOF-based MMMs are still in limit for flue and biogas separation applications. Surprisingly, most of the developed MOF-based MMMs are mainly based on a few well-known MOFs such as HKUST-1, MILs, ZIFs, $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ , UiO-66, MCM-41, and so on. It should be noted that most of the cases permeability versus selectivity trade-off was maintained due to the presence of surface functionality in the MOFs as discussed previously. To analyze the exact potentiality of the developed MOF-based MMMs, we have plotted permeability versus separation selectivity of the developed MMMs till date, as depicted in Figure 55. Although $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})/\mathrm{PI}$ for flue gas (Figure  55a) and branched HKUST-1/6FDA-DAM for biogas (Figure 55b) exhibited relatively better performance over others, they are still far from Robeson upper bound limit and demands enormous focus on effective design and development in future. Although, depending upon the number of stages and other process conditions the targeted selectivity value can be varied, for $90\%$ post-combustion $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture and for economical adsorption-based separation at least 20 to $200\mathrm{~CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ separation selectivity with a permeance of 1000–10000 GPU is required.[499] The increment of surface area can not only enhance the permeability but also linearly enhance the cost of capture and make the material inconvenient as our target is to design and develop cost-effective high performance adsorption-based MMMs. Additionally, the developed materials should possess long term stability and will be viable for easy scalability in terms of technically and economically. Unfortunately, membrane-based flue and biogas separations are less explored compared to adsorption-based separation process and it is believed that the results obtained so far with regard to trade-off between permeability versus selectivity will encourage the researchers to explore the area further in order to achieve the Robeson upper bound limit.
## 9. Conclusions and Future Prospects
In conclusion, we have portrayed the potential of flue and biogas separation by MOFs while organizing several approaches taken by the researchers and enlisted the progress in Table 3, 4 (Table S1 and S2 in Supporting Information) and Table 7 for a rapid visualization. All the developed strategies were exclusively and critically discussed with the corresponding figures by selecting suitable MOFs. For example, IITKGP-11, IISERPMOF2, Qc-5-Cu-sql- $\beta$ $\mathrm{{Zn}_{2}(L)}$ (1), $\mathrm{Mn}_{13}(\mathrm{L})$ (2), MOF-888-891, NJU-Bai32, NJU-Bai35, Cu-1, UTSA-90, and ZJNU-26-29 were discussed under the light of pore-confinement effect for selectivity enhancement driven by either framework interpenetration or framework non-interpenetration, whereas PCN-88, NJU-Bai33, and ZJU-197 displayed enhanced selectivity due to presence of high-density OMSs. To discuss surface functionalization effect in selectivity triggering, we have classified this section into three parts and discussed exclusively with the examples such as selectivity enhancement for 1  versus $^{1-}$ $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , Zn-CBS, IITKGP-12, ZU-36-Ni, BUT-10, BUT-11, UTSA121, UTSA-93, were driven by functional groups only, whereas IITKGP-7, IITKGP-8, FJU-44 were driven by bared heteroatoms and IITKGP-5, IITKGP-6, UTSA-49, SNNU-13−16 were driven by multiple functional sites. Selectivity triggers through charged skeleton was exemplified by In-MOF (1), Zn-MOF (1), $\mathtt{L i-Z n}$ cluster-based MOFs, 1 and 2, whereas 1•S, $\mathrm{\Gamma[Cu(bcppm)H_{2}O]}\cdot x S$ M-btz-as, $\mathrm{[Zn_{2}(L)_{2}]}\cdot2\mathrm{DMA}\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (1), polycatenated MOFs, 1 and 2, HMOF (2) displayed enhanced selectivity due to framework flexibility. The separation selectivity also triggered via PSP approach (exemplified by $\mathrm{PN}@\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}5$ , SNNU-51–56), combined approach (exemplified by CSMCRI-9, NJU-Bai52, NJUBai53, ZJNU-43-45, 1·DMF, 1·DMA, UPC-70, PMOF 1, PMOF 2, 1a, CSMCRI-3, NENU-520, FJI-H29, NJU-Bai7 and NJU-Bai8), and composite formations (exemplified by UTSA-16–GO, $\mathsf{M O F-}505@\mathsf{G O}$ , and $\mathrm{Ni}@Z\mathrm{rOF},$ ). Furthermore, to have a better insight and to analyze step by step sorption mechanism, MOFs with in situ experiments (MAF-stu-1, UTSA-120, SCNU-1, NJUBai50, NJU-Bai51, 1a, 1b, and MAF-X7) were discussed in the “Elucidation of sorption mechanism through in situ experimental study” section exclusively. Also, considering the urgency and necessity of MOF-based MMMs in flue and biogas separation a comprehensive discussion has been made with suitable examples and corresponding figures. To end, the current status of the developed MOFs and MOF-based MMMs toward these directions along with their effectivity comparison were made and discussed exclusively in the “Critical Analysis” section.
As reviewed herein, although MOFs displayed splendid promise for flue and biogas separations, there are quite a few underdeveloped issues or challenges, which need to explore more, not only for obtaining maximum potentiality but also to keep MOF one hand upper than other developed porous materials. These are
## 9.1. Synthesis Sophistications
By thorough investigation we found that except ZU-36-Ni, all the MOFs were synthesized mainly based on solvothermal synthesis or room temperature synthesis. Solvothermal synthesis and room temperature crystallization are undoubtedly good choices but for scale up to the industrial level, other easier methods should be taken care of,[236] especially mechanochemical synthesis or microwave synthesis. ZU-36-Ni was synthesized mechanochemically, which is really necessary and demanding, as this is a cleaner synthesis route for chemical transformations. Additionally, precursors having lower solubility or no solubility often becomes a major problem during crystallization, on such cases, mechanochemical synthesis would be an excellent method as this synthesis sustain by only mechanical force (ball milling assisted) and can be performed with a very little or no solvent presence. Moreover, for G-scale synthesis microwave synthesis or overnight reflux method is very useful and need to employ toward MOF construction more effectively. In addition, considering the economic and environmental viability, green synthesis approach should be considered for MOF construction. Therefore, the synthesis sophistication yet not been achieved and demands intensive attention for further development.
## 9.2. Stability Issue
To date, a limited number of MOFs only displayed water and/or moisture stability or excellent acid-base resistivity and retained their potentiality toward these projected flue and biogas separation applications in practical conditions. The moisture stability is the prerequisite criteria for these separation applications, as on-site flue gas mixture often contains $8\mathrm{-}10\%$ of moisture (water vapor). Same is true for biogas separation as well $(5-10\%)$ . The existence of water in these gas mixture have a detrimental effect in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ selectivity due to the competitive adsorption between $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and water molecules. Hence, the developed frameworks need to be acid-base resistive as flue and biogas contain several toxic gases such as $\mathrm{SO}_{x}$ $\left\langle\mathrm{SO}_{2}\right\rangle$ and $\mathrm{SO}_{3})$ , $\mathsf{N O}_{x}$ (NO and $\mathrm{NO}_{2};$ ), $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ , which are corrosive in nature and can degrade the framework during cyclic operations. It should be noted that the potential competition between the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules and these acidic gas uptake is still unclear, which demands enormous attention of the researchers. Additionally, in terms of practicality both the working capacity and diffusion kinetics need to be considered within the dynamic adsorption process. Unfortunately, most of the adsorption-based materials were designed and developed considering equilibrium adsorption capacities, while a lack of attention was paid in designing those materials by considering the kinetics of adsorption.[499] However, presence of water molecules can also be beneficial for enhanced $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption in some cases by providing additional interaction sites through a carbonate formation, where a small amount of water (approximately $4\mathrm{wt\%}$ ) molecules coordinated within the matrix of open–metal site MOFs. Hence, design and development of an adsorbent is also pronounced in which water is adsorbed and becomes more basic, and thus more likely to react with the acidic carbon atom of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , would lead to a cooperative effect between water and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake. Design and development of adsorbents having hydrophobic surface of course will provide a better framework stability but presence of these additional surface groups will block the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption sites and/or pore volume, which will further reduce the maximum $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity. Hence, for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture, in the case of physical adsorption approaches pre-removal of water from the gas mixtures may be a necessary step and demanding too. Although in FGD (Flue Gas Desulfurization) process, almost $95\%$ of $\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ can be removed from the flue gas emitted of coal-fired power plants, (which is very energy intensive and cost-effective (\$60 to $\$100/\mathrm{k w}$ for the limestone and $\$94$ to $\$145/\mathrm{kw}$ for the sodium solution scrubbing systems),[501] the rest of the sulfur content $(5\%)$ after FDG process can significantly damage the MOF materials and thus MOFs must be stable in mild acidic environments. To enhance the moisture stability and acid-base resistivity quite a few strategies have been proposed,[90,92,93,100] however, the progress is still limited and demands more attention for further development.
## 9.3. Defects of MOFs
Although it is conceptually true that an ideal crystal structure of MOF possesses perfectly coordinated metal ions and organic ligands to construct a network, in reality, MOFs possess uncoordinated metal center and ligands or vacancies in the crystal structure, which is known as defects. The defects in MOFs are defined as “sites that locally break the regular periodic arrangement of atoms or ions of the static crystalline parent framework because of missing or dislocated atoms or ions”.[502] Defects in MOFs’ structure have a significant impact on tuning their functional properties, which further enhance the performance in various applications, as pioneered by several groups.[503–505] MOFs’ defect formation could be categorized into two types: (a) missing linkers which could also be termed as “metal defect” and (b) missing metal also termed as “ligand defect”.[502] Although defects are the inherent property of MOFs, they could also be created intentionally by employing several synthesis strategies such as mixed ligand synthesis, use of modulators, postsynthetic modification, and so on.[505] As metal ions and linkers act as the active sites of developed MOFs, their defects tune the structural properties and influence their applications. It should be noted that structural defects in MOFs deal with the deviation from the perfect structure but it does not mean severe decline or collapse of the crystal structure. As the influence of defects with electronic balance generates larger cavities in the crystal and increases the void space, the presence of defects in MOFs enhances the performance of adsorptive efficiency. Considering this, to obtain the enhanced gas sorption performance reports on defective MOFs synthesis are increasing swiftly. However, to improve the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture by defective MOFs, several strategies were adopted such as (a) coordinatively unsaturated metal site introduction, (b) H-bonding residues incorporation, and (c) basic site functionalization.[502] Additionally, partial removal of organic linkers can also enhance the porosity of the MOFs and lead to open access to the previously inaccessible regions, which further create new interaction sites and improve the sorption capacity. In recent work, Navarro et  al. nicely displayed how strategically developed defective MOF can exhibit improved $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture from flue gas mixture.[504] By post-synthetic treatment, defective MOFs, $1@\mathrm{KOH}$ , $2@\mathrm{KOH}$ , and $3@\mathrm{KOH}$ , were developed, which lead to the partial removal of organic linkers, coordinated water molecules’ deprotonation, phenolate basic sites generation, and extra framework cations introduction. As a result, enhanced $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity with increased adsorption heat was obtained, which further made the developed defective MOF as a superior candidate for flue gas separation. However, understanding and controlling the defect formation of MOFs is still a challenge and demands immense attention in future development. Although to understand the equilibrium control between metal and ligand defects several conventional methods were employed, the molecular insight is still unclear and demands extensive efforts in this direction.
## 9.4. Natural gas Post-Combustion Flue Gas Separation
Enormous efforts had been devoted to analyze the separation performance of MOFs toward flue gas mixture generated from coal-fired power plant, as reviewed in this paper. However, less to no attention had been paid to separate flue gas mixture generated from natural gas post-combustion, which is more challenging than the flue gas mixture generated from coal-fired power plant as it contains relatively less amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ despite having almost similar of $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ concentration (Table 1). It is now expected from the researchers to study and report both sorts of flue gas separations in near future.
## 9.5. Shaping of MOFs
Although MOFs have attracted significant attention in various applications as mentioned earlier, the direct use of powdered MOFs generated from the conventional synthesis methods is not possible in the adsorption separation process due to having several limitations such as low mass transfer rate, high pressure drop, and poor recyclability with scattering tendencies.[3,506,507] Thus, for industrial applications, adsorbents need shaping in different morphology such as tablets, palates, batons, agglomerate, etc., which might be taken care of into consideration as there is a possibility of losing its potentiality during changing its shape. The shaping processes are generally accompanied by varying degrees of performance degradation of the original MOFs material, which further leads to the loss of its adsorption capacity by a decrease of the specific surface area and porosity. Therefore, shaping of MOF monoliths into the appropriate shapes without no significant loss of original properties (porosity, uptake capacity, mechanical strength, stability etc.) is on demand. As almost every practical application requires specific devices with right shapes, it is necessary to combine the structural diversity and performance of MOFs with industrial practice. For example, MOF powders need to be shaped either in the form of spherical beads or in the form of cylindrical pellets to evaluate their practical performance in adsorption-based process such as PSA.[508] However, a certain decrease in working capacity could be observed for the palletized sample as the physical properties are affected due to use of binder. Although, for adsorption, in the presence of the binder the chemical properties, which influence the separation selectivity should not change, mass transfer through the pellets should be low.[509] It is also reported that during the static and dynamic adsorption processes such as PSA, TSA, and ESA (electric swing adsorption), the shaped adsorbents facilitates more mass transfer and gaseous diffusion across the adsorbent as well. Therefore, in parallel with the development of various synthesis methods, it is also equally important to shape the MOFs in a right way to suit the specific industrial operation. Although recently $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorptive MOFs has been shaped into several forms such as pellets, films, hollow structures, granules, and honeycomb monoliths, implementation and potentiality check by shaping of MOF into extrusion molding, direct coating, 3D printing, and mechanical pressing form is yet to be investigated.[506] Thus, an extensive effort is required to optimize the performance of developed MOFs in proper shaped form.
## 9.6. Computer-assisted Screening
As mentioned earlier, to date among all the developed MOFs, maximum are employed in gas sorption and separations application including these challenging flue and biogas separations. Thus, for rapid screening, determination of the preferential binding sites, binding possibility and probability from the large database using the state-of-art molecular simulation methodologies will be a righteousness practice in the partnership of experimental work.[510–513]
The past few decades witnessed significant progressive research and development toward efficient $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture technologies and flue and biogas separations, among which MOFs proved their promise as an adsorbent over other developed porous materials, however, they are still far from the economically appealing commercialization. Enormous research efforts had been made from time to time on the methods to separate the flue gas and elevate the biogas to a higher degree of bio-methane. Interestingly, it is possible to close the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ loop with parallel production of bio-methane and bio- $\mathrm{.CO}_{2}$ during biogas separation process. The basic scientific research has been always developed first before the subsequent commercialization of the materials for wider usage. It is true for MOF materials as well, even the field has been established for almost three decades, some practical industrial applications of MOFs just started coming now. By observing the speedy advancement in effective design and construction of MOFs in academia, there are every reasons to believe that MOFs indeed have massive potential to overcome all the challenges soon, through immense collaborative engagement with industry by adopting the views of process system engineering (PSE) to ensure its bright future toward practical flue and biogas separation applications domains.
